export const KNOWLEDGE = "# Arcus\n\n> Arcus is the truth layer for marketing leaders, founders and executives at entertainment and lifestyle brands. It connects the data every team already has, from sales, CRM and player or viewer data to social and agency reports, into one layer the brand owns, adds what audiences say across the internet, checks every claim, and answers any question with the whole picture. It doesn’t make ads, buy media or take a cut of spend.\n\nArcus (usearcus.ai) is for in-house marketing teams at studios, streamers, spirits and beverage, games, music, fashion and beauty brands, especially teams without their own data department. Brands’ data is usually in pieces: sales in one system, players or viewers in another, email and CRM with a third team, plus retail data, ad accounts, social accounts, agency reports and listening tools. Arcus connects all of it into one layer inside the brand’s own setup (about three months, starting with one free question), and the brand owns it. After that, any leader can ask any question and get an answer that sees the whole picture, delivered as a few slides with the answer on top. It is not an agency, a dashboard, a listening tool or a survey.\n\n## What Arcus does\n- [Check what worked](https://usearcus.ai/how-it-works#check): every result the brand’s marketing claims, from agencies, platforms or creators, is checked against the brand’s own numbers (sales, tickets, sign-ups, streams) and counted once; anything budget moves on gets a regions-on, regions-off test\n- [What people say, next to what they did](https://usearcus.ai/listening): works with any listening tool the brand already has, and reads and counts public conversation on Reddit, TikTok, YouTube, Instagram, X, Threads, Bluesky, Facebook groups, Discord, Twitch, Kick, Google Search, Goodreads, Fable and more, lined up with the brand’s first-party data\n- [What to do next](https://usearcus.ai/reports): which posts, creators and channels move people, and what to keep, test and stop\n- [Connect everything](https://usearcus.ai/how-it-works#build): connects every system the brand runs (sales, players or viewers, email and CRM, retail, ads, social, agency reports, listening tools) into one layer the brand owns, inside its own setup; no new software for the team\n- [Ask anything](https://usearcus.ai/how-it-works#report): any leader can ask any question; answers land in Slack, email, Sheets or the brand’s dashboard\n\n## Start\n- [First question, free](https://usearcus.ai/free-report): bring one marketing question; Arcus answers it with the brand’s own numbers and what its audience says, with a 30-minute walkthrough; free, no card\n- [Pricing](https://usearcus.ai/pricing): first question free, then a one-time setup fee and a monthly fee\n- [Book a call](https://usearcus.ai/book)\n\n## Real answers (names removed)\n- [Content report: A hard seltzer brand](https://usearcus.ai/reports/content-report): We sorted every Instagram and Facebook post from one summer campaign by what was on screen, then scored each kind against the brand’s own account numbers.\n- [Audience report: A streaming service](https://usearcus.ai/reports/audience-report): We counted what men 18 to 24 post and comment on TikTok, Reddit, YouTube, X and Instagram, then ran the same count on the brand’s own posts.\n- [Market report: A skincare brand moving into retail](https://usearcus.ai/reports/market-report): We traced five direct-to-consumer brands that scaled into retail through the public record, set the brand’s own public numbers against them, and sourced every number.\n- [Agency check (example numbers)](https://usearcus.ai/reports/agency-check): We line up each agency’s claims against your own numbers, whether that’s sales, tickets, sign-ups or streams, and show which ones hold up.\n\n## Who it’s for\n- [Spirits and beverage](https://usearcus.ai/for/spirits-and-beverage): Find out which videos sold cases.\n- [Studios and streaming](https://usearcus.ai/for/studios-and-streaming): Find out which trailers and posts filled seats.\n- [Games and music](https://usearcus.ai/for/games-and-music): Find out which creators sold copies, and which sold themselves.\n- [Fashion and beauty](https://usearcus.ai/for/fashion-and-beauty): Find out which drops your marketing actually sold.\n\n## Comparisons\n- [Arcus vs. Your agency’s own reports](https://usearcus.ai/compare/agency-reports): Your agency’s report is still the best record of what they ran and when. Keep it. Just don’t let it be the only check.\n- [Arcus vs. Analytics software](https://usearcus.ai/compare/analytics-software): If your analysts want a tool to explore in, keep it. The layer we build is yours, and their software can sit on top of it.\n- [Arcus vs. Hiring a data team](https://usearcus.ai/compare/hiring-a-data-team): If data is core to how your company competes, build the team. Arcus can be the bridge while you hire.\n- [Arcus vs. HAUS](https://usearcus.ai/compare/haus): If your team runs paid media in-house and wants to run its own lift tests, HAUS is built for that.\n- [Arcus vs. Hex](https://usearcus.ai/compare/hex): If you have analysts, Hex is a strong tool for them, and it can sit on top of the layer we build.\n- [Arcus vs. IRIS](https://usearcus.ai/compare/iris): If you run a performance team that wants a measurement tool of its own, IRIS may be built for you [Check on their site].\n- [Arcus vs. Northbeam](https://usearcus.ai/compare/northbeam): If you sell mostly online and have a performance team that wants an attribution dashboard, Northbeam is built for that.\n- [Arcus vs. Agency audit firms](https://usearcus.ai/compare/agency-audit-firms): If your main worry is fees, rates, billing or contract terms, a media audit firm is the right call.\n\n## Guides\n- [Is your agency double counting your sales?](https://usearcus.ai/library/agency-double-counting-sales): If you use more than one agency, probably. Every ad platform credits itself when its ad touched a buyer, so one sale can show up in two or three agency reports. To check, add up every agency’s claims for one month and compare the total with your own numbers for the same month.\n- [How to read an agency report in 10 minutes](https://usearcus.ai/library/read-an-agency-report): Skip the impressions and engagement totals. Find the results the agency claims, how it counted them (clicks or views, and over how many days), what it spent, and how much credit went to people already searching your name. Then compare the claims with your own numbers for the same dates.\n- [12 questions to ask your agency before you renew](https://usearcus.ai/library/questions-before-you-renew): Ask how they count a sale, over how many days, how much credit goes to brand search and view-throughs, whether their results overlap with your other agencies, and what they would test if the budget were cut. Good agencies answer clearly, share the detail behind each claim, and welcome a test.\n- [What is first-party data, and what do I do with mine?](https://usearcus.ai/library/first-party-data-for-brand-marketers): First-party data is what your company collects itself: sales, tickets, sign-ups, viewing, customers, email, your own social accounts and site visits. It tells you which posts, campaigns and agencies line up with what actually happened, without relying on what platforms or agencies say. Start with one question and the data you already hold that touches it.\n- [Incrementality, explained without the math](https://usearcus.ai/library/incrementality-without-the-math): Incrementality means the results that happened because of a campaign and wouldn’t have happened without it. The clearest way to measure it is a holdout test: run the campaign in some regions and not others for a few weeks, then compare. The difference is the campaign’s effect.\n- [Social listening that counts, not guesses](https://usearcus.ai/library/social-listening-that-counts): Useful listening counts specific things in your audience’s own words: what they post, what they reward, which words they use, and what they never mention. Then it checks those patterns against your own numbers. Sentiment scores and word clouds rarely change a decision. Counts and real quotes do.\n- [How to tell which of your posts work](https://usearcus.ai/library/which-posts-actually-work): Group your posts by what happens on screen, then compare each group’s median views and shares with your account average. Watched and shared are different jobs, so check both. Mark groups of fewer than four posts as directional, and end with what to keep, test and stop.\n- [Do you need a data team? A checklist](https://usearcus.ai/library/do-you-need-a-data-team): You need a dedicated data team if data is core to how your company competes and you have new questions every week. If you mainly need trustworthy answers about agencies, content and your audience a few times a month, a setup built inside the tools you already use, and owned by you, is usually faster than hiring and cheaper than a team.\n- [What a 42x view gap says about your brand voice](https://usearcus.ai/library/what-a-42x-view-gap-says-about-brand-voice): It says the audience rewards a joke it can join, so a brand voice should play along with their bits rather than preach or start its own. In a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, an acted-out bit had a median of 91,850 views against 2,171 for a sincere post and 1,675 for a brand post. Viewers liked the sincere posts at the same rate per view; they reached 42 times fewer people.\n- [How young audiences write, counted](https://usearcus.ai/library/how-young-audiences-write): Short, first person and almost never with a hashtag. In a real Arcus audience report, men 18 to 24 wrote five-word lines, used one slang word in every 62, and put a hashtag in 0.2% of comments, while the brand’s own captions carried one 92.5% of the time. Write shorter, drop the hashtags, and leave the slang to them.\n- [Facebook watches but doesn’t share. What to post where](https://usearcus.ai/library/facebook-watches-but-doesnt-share): In one brand’s data, Facebook audiences watched useful, repeatable tricks and almost never passed anything on. In a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, Facebook posts averaged 1.2 shares each, 24 of 29 posts got zero or one, and the biggest post on either platform, a how-to with 37,836 views, got none. Post watchable tricks on both platforms, and give Instagram the friend-group jokes made to be sent.\n- [Watched or shared? The hosting hack pattern and the share-rate math](https://usearcus.ai/library/watched-vs-shared-share-rate): Views tell you a post got watched; shares tell you someone sent it on, and the two reward different things. In a real Arcus content report, hosting hacks led Instagram views at 2.4 times the average but sat below average on shares, while a joke about the viewer’s friends took 409 shares on 8,000 views. Read shares per 1,000 views next to views, because a high rate on a small post can mean very little.\n- [Does retail eat online sales? What five brands that moved into retail show](https://usearcus.ai/library/does-retail-eat-online-sales): Not at any of the five brands in a real Arcus case series: Hero, Bubble, Harry’s, Liquid Death and Poppi. The online share of sales shifted as retail grew, but demand didn’t move away; industry research across $848 billion of transactions found a new retail location lifts nearby online sales 6.9%, and 13.9% for direct-to-consumer brands. What retail does eat is margin: the retailer’s cut, trade spend, slotting and deductions.\n- [How to check your Meta agency (Facebook and Instagram)](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-meta-agency): Start by asking for Meta’s results split into clicks and views, with the window behind each. Then add Meta’s claims to every other agency’s and compare the total with your own numbers. For proof that Meta caused results, run ads in some regions and not others for a few weeks.\n- [How to check your TikTok agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-tiktok-agency): Ask for TikTok’s results split into clicks and views, with the window behind each, and a list of any boosted creator posts. Add TikTok’s claims to every other agency’s and compare the total with your own numbers. To prove TikTok caused results, hold out some regions for a few weeks.\n- [How to check your Google Search agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-google-search-agency): Ask your search agency to split results into searches for your brand’s name and everything else. Brand search mostly catches people already on their way to you. To find out what it adds, pause brand search in some regions for a few weeks and compare results with your own numbers.\n- [How to check your YouTube agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-youtube-agency): Ask your YouTube agency which results came from people who clicked and which from people who only watched, and over what window. Ask whether the view counts include paid views. Then add YouTube’s claims to every other agency’s and compare with your own numbers.\n- [How to check your Snapchat agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-snapchat-agency): Ask your agency for Snapchat’s results split into swipes or clicks and views, with the window behind each. Snapchat often reaches the same people as TikTok and Instagram, so add every platform’s claims together and compare the total with your own numbers. Hold out some regions to prove cause.\n- [How to check your Reddit agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-reddit-agency): Check a Reddit recap in two parts. First, match Reddit’s claimed results against your own numbers and every other agency’s claims, with clicks and views split. Second, read the comments on your promoted posts and the threads around them. Reddit users talk back, and that tells you how the ads landed.\n- [How to check your Pinterest agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-pinterest-agency): Pinterest is a planning platform, so results often come days or weeks after someone saves a Pin. That makes the window behind each result the first thing to check. Then split clicks from views, add Pinterest’s claims to every other agency’s, and compare the total with your own numbers.\n- [How to check your LinkedIn agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-linkedin-agency): Match every lead, registration or sign-up LinkedIn claims against your own records, so each person is counted once. Split results from clicks and from views, and check the window. Then follow the leads forward: what matters is how many became something, which your CRM can show.\n- [How to check your creator and influencer agency](https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-creator-agency): Judge creators on results in your own numbers. Reach and estimated media value come second. Check every code and link for coupon-site leaks, match creator claims against your media agency’s claims for boosted posts, and line up post dates with results. Hold out regions on paid boosts to prove cause.\n\n## Glossary\n- [Attribution](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/attribution): Deciding which ad, post or channel gets credit for a sale.\n- [Incrementality](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/incrementality): The sales that happened because of a campaign and wouldn’t have happened without it.\n- [Double counting](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/double-counting): When two or more agencies or platforms each take credit for the same sale.\n- [Holdout test](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/holdout-test): A test where a campaign runs in some places and not others, so you can compare.\n- [ROAS](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/roas): Return on ad spend: sales credited to ads, divided by what the ads cost.\n- [View-through conversion](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/view-through-conversion): A sale credited to an ad that someone saw but didn’t click.\n- [Conversion window](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/conversion-window): How many days after seeing or clicking an ad a sale still counts for it.\n- [Last-click attribution](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/last-click): Giving all the credit to the last thing someone clicked before buying.\n- [Brand search](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/brand-search): Ads shown to people who search your brand’s name.\n- [Creator code](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/creator-code): A discount code or link given to a creator so sales can be traced to them.\n- [First-party data](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/first-party-data): Data your company collects itself: sales, tickets, sign-ups, viewing, customers, email, your own social accounts.\n- [Third-party data](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/third-party-data): Data from outside your company: public conversation, search trends, reviews, market research.\n- [Social listening](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/social-listening): Reading what people say about a brand, product or topic across social media and the web.\n- [Media mix modeling](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/media-mix-modeling): A statistical model that estimates how much each channel contributes to sales over time.\n- [Share rate](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/share-rate): How often people who saw a post sent it on: shares divided by views.\n- [Engagement rate](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/engagement-rate): Likes, comments, shares and saves on a post, divided by the people who saw it or by follower count.\n- [Reach vs. impressions](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/reach-vs-impressions): Reach is how many different people saw something. Impressions is how many times it was shown, counting repeats.\n- [Brand lift study](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/brand-lift-study): A survey that compares people who saw a campaign with people who didn’t, to measure changes in awareness, recall or intent.\n- [Retail media](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/retail-media): Ads sold by retailers on their own websites and apps, measured against the retailer’s own sales data.\n- [Sell-through and depletions](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/sell-through): Sell-through is how much product shoppers buy at retail. Depletions are how much distributors sell on to retailers, bars and restaurants.\n- [Creator whitelisting](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/creator-whitelisting): Running paid ads through a creator’s account, so the ad appears under their name. Also called allowlisting or partnership ads.\n- [Sentiment](https://usearcus.ai/glossary/sentiment): A score for whether conversation about a brand is positive, negative or neutral.\n\n## Company\n- [About](https://usearcus.ai/about)\n- [Security](https://usearcus.ai/security)\n- [FAQ](https://usearcus.ai/faq)\n- [Nazeem Ahmed, Chief AI Officer](https://usearcus.ai/about/nazeem-ahmed)\n- [Min Kwak, Chief Revenue Officer](https://usearcus.ai/about/min-kwak)\n- [Devin Min, Chief Product Officer](https://usearcus.ai/about/devin-min)\n- [Brendon Ghaderi, Chief Technology Officer](https://usearcus.ai/about/brendon-ghaderi)\n\n## Optional\n- [Full text for AI assistants](https://usearcus.ai/llms-full.txt)\n\n\n---\n\n# Arcus, full text\n\nArcus is the truth layer for marketing leaders, founders and executives at entertainment and lifestyle brands. It connects the data every team already has, from sales, CRM and player or viewer data to social and agency reports, into one layer the brand owns, adds what audiences say across the internet, checks every claim, and answers any question with the whole picture. It doesn’t make ads, buy media or take a cut of spend.\n\n# Reports\n\n## Content report: Tricks with the can get watched. Posts about the viewer’s friends get shared.\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/reports/content-report\nFor a hard seltzer brand, names removed.\n39 Instagram posts and 29 Facebook posts over 13 weeks, from the brand’s own social export.\n\n- The top line: Hosting hacks get watched. Median 19,188 views on Instagram against an account average of 8,148. Leads on Facebook too. Four posts, the minimum for a read, and the same on both platforms.\n- What worked: A trick with the can you could copy tonight. The three biggest posts show the can frozen, disguised or turned into dessert. Each one shows a step you can repeat at home.\n- What didn’t: Telling people to quit is not a bit. Anti-work jokes played straight stayed under 2,300 views each. Unusual places with nothing happening in them were the weakest named pattern on the account.\n- Where shares come from: The joke is about the viewer’s friends. One post took 409 shares on 8,000 views: 51 per thousand. Nothing else on the account broke 12. It was about a moment people were in, not about the product.\n- Facebook: Facebook watches. It doesn’t share. 1.2 shares per post on average, and 24 of 29 posts at zero or one. It does watch: one how-to did 11.7 times the Facebook average and zero shares.\n- What to do:  Keep: Hosting hacks. They lead on Instagram and on Facebook. Test: A friend-group frame on the formats that already work, and watch the share rate. Stop: 3D animation, and the anti-work idea as written.\n\n## Audience report: They write in five-word lines, and they talk about the shows, not the service.\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/reports/audience-report\nFor a streaming service, names removed.\n293 TikToks sorted by hand, 2,965 comments measured by script, 480 labeled by hand, 6,622 counted for words, 4,283 Reddit questions and 78,682 comments under four trailers.\n\n- How they write: They write five-word lines. Brands write hashtags. A typical line is five words, first person, lower case. One word in 62 is slang. The brand’s own captions carried a hashtag 92.5% of the time; their comments, 0.2%.\n- What gets watched: A bit gets 42 times the views of a sincere post. Median 91,850 views for an acted-out sketch, 2,171 for a sincere post, 1,675 for a brand post. Per viewer, both get liked at the same rate. The sincere post just reaches 42 times fewer people.\n- What they reward: Half of the top comments are jokes. Of the 360 most-liked comments, 49% are a joke of some kind. One in ten is sincere. Sarcasm is rare, and when it lands it is the second most-liked kind.\n- Their words, counted: They don’t use the glossary. The two most searched slang terms of 2025 appear in one comment and in none, out of 6,622. The words they do use are verdicts on the work, not decoration.\n- What they talk about: They talk about the shows, not the service. 78,682 comments under four trailers. The service only comes up when it gets in the way: ads, the app, a cancelled show.\n- What to do:  Play along: Reply to the bit. Don’t start one. Tell, don’t ask: State a time. Never ask the room a question. Hand over the mic: Let a fan’s line be the caption. No glossary: No slang in the caption.\n\n## Market report: Retail didn’t eat online sales at any of five brands that made the move. It took margin.\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/reports/market-report\nFor a skincare brand moving into retail, names removed.\nFive brands, ten slides, a 157-source ledger and 14 source slides showing the page behind each number.\n\n- The question: Will retail eat online sales? Five brands that moved from selling online to thousands of stores. What happened to their own sites, and what the media did.\n- What happened: Retail didn’t eat online at any of the five. The share shifted; the online business grew. The cannibalization that is real is margin: the retailer’s cut, trade spend and fees. Not demand.\n- Why: The two channels fed each other. A store opening lifts online sales nearby by 6.9%, and 13.9% for direct-to-consumer brands, according to industry research across $848 billion of transactions.\n- What the media did: Media pointed at one shelf, in one week. The launches that worked sent people to a named store in its launch week, then reported that store’s sales, not impressions.\n- What to do:  Point: Launch media at one retailer’s shelf in its set week. Fund: Retail media from the trade budget, for position. Protect: Give the site a job the shelf can’t do. Report: The four numbers buyers manage to.\n\n## Agency check: Three agencies claimed 4,200 results. In this example, 1,380 held up.\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/reports/agency-check\nExample numbers.\nYour agencies’ reports, your own numbers (sales, tickets, sign-ups or viewing), and the ad accounts the reports cover.\n\n- The headline: They said 4,200. We found 1,380. Example numbers. Each claim is checked against your own numbers. 2,820 didn’t hold up.\n- How we match: Every claim, counted once. Example. Each sale, ticket or sign-up an agency claims is checked against your own numbers by date, title or product, and channel. Each one can be matched once. If two agencies claim it, it goes to neither until a test says otherwise.\n- Where the rest went: 2,820 claims didn’t hold up. Example. 1,610 were counted by two agencies at once. 740 came from your own emails. 470 came from people already searching your name.\n- Agency by agency:  Social: Claimed 2,100. Matched 820. Search: Claimed 1,300. Matched 310, most of it brand search. Creators: Claimed 800. Matched 250, after coupon-site codes.\n- What we’d test: Pause brand search in half your regions for three weeks. Example. If sales hold in the paused regions, that budget can go somewhere that earns it. If they drop, you know the channel works, and you know by how much.\n- What to do:  Ask: Take the list of claims that didn’t hold up to your next agency review. Test: For anything you’d move budget on, run a regions-on, regions-off test. Renew: Keep the agencies whose claims hold up. You’ll know which ones.\n\n# FAQ\n\nQ: What is Arcus?\nA: Arcus is the truth layer for marketing leaders, founders and executives at entertainment and lifestyle brands. It connects the data every team already has, from sales, CRM and player or viewer data to social and agency reports, into one layer the brand owns, adds what audiences say across the internet, checks every claim, and answers any question with the whole picture. It doesn’t make ads, buy media or take a cut of spend.\n\nQ: Who is Arcus for?\nA: In-house marketing teams at studios, streamers, drinks, games, music, fashion and beauty brands. Especially teams without their own data department.\n\nQ: Is Arcus software or a service?\nA: Both. Arcus is our own product, and our engineers set it up inside your systems and run it with you. You don’t get a login and a manual: we connect your data into one place inside your own setup and keep it running. You ask questions, and answers land in Slack, email, Sheets or your dashboard. There’s nothing new to log into, and the layer is yours to keep.\n\nQ: What do you connect?\nA: Every system that holds a piece of your marketing: sales, tickets or sign-ups; players, viewers or app usage; email, CRM and customer data; retail and partner data; your ad and social accounts; your agencies’ reports; and any listening tool you already use. It all goes into one layer you own.\n\nQ: Do we need a data team?\nA: No. We do the data work. You get the answers.\n\nQ: Do you look at what people say online?\nA: Yes. We read Reddit, TikTok, YouTube, X, Instagram, Facebook groups, Discord, Twitch, Kick, Google Search, Goodreads, Fable, reviews and more, count what comes up, and line it up with your own sales and customer data.\n\nQ: What do we get from the free question?\nA: One question, answered with your own numbers and what people say: whether an agency’s results actually happened, which of your posts work, what your audience says, or something else you need to know. You get a few slides, answer on top, ending in what to keep, test and stop, plus a 30-minute walkthrough.\n\nQ: What do you need from us?\nA: Your question, and access to the numbers it needs from your own systems (sales, tickets, sign-ups or viewing). For an agency question, also the agency report and the ad accounts it covers. We walk you through it.\n\nQ: Is it really free?\nA: Yes. No card.\n\nQ: How long does setup take?\nA: About three months from kickoff to everything connected. You approve each step, and answers arrive along the way.\n\nQ: What kinds of reports do you send?\nA: Most answers fall into four kinds: agency checks, content reports, audience reports and market reports. You can ask anything else too. Each answer is a few slides with the answer on top and what to keep, test and stop at the end.\n\nQ: How often do reports come?\nA: On a schedule you choose, or whenever you have a question.\n\nQ: Do we have to change agencies?\nA: No. Arcus works alongside your agencies and holds them to your own numbers. Sometimes that shows an agency is doing great work, and you’ll know that too.\n\nQ: How much does it cost?\nA: Your first question is free. After that there’s a one-time setup fee and a monthly fee. The Pricing page has the details.\n\nQ: Is this the same as attribution?\nA: No. Matching claims against your own numbers removes double counting. When you need to know whether something caused a sale, we run a simple test: show it in some regions and not others, then compare.\n\nQ: Who checks the AI?\nA: A person on our team reads every report before you do and writes up what it means. The AI can only do jobs we’ve approved, and every step is logged.\n\nQ: We don’t sell online. Is Arcus still for us?\nA: Yes. Arcus works from whatever your results are: tickets, streams, sign-ups, viewing, retail sales or distributor depletions. We match agency claims against those numbers, count each result once, and tell you where the data is too thin to call.\n\nQ: Most of our sales happen at retail. Can you still check agency claims?\nA: Yes. We line campaigns up with the sales data you have, whether that’s retailer data, scan data or depletions from distributors, by week and by region. Retail data usually arrives later and in less detail, so some answers need a regions-on, regions-off test, and we’ll say when.\n\nQ: Why line up listening with our own numbers?\nA: Conversation on its own tells you what people say. Your own numbers tell you what they do. Lined up, you can see which topics, formats and creators move sales, tickets, sign-ups or streams, and which ones only get talked about.\n\nQ: Which agency report should we send?\nA: If your first question is about an agency, send the report you’re least sure about, or the one with a renewal or budget decision coming up. A recent monthly or campaign recap works best, with the dates and the claimed results in it.\n\nQ: What if our numbers are messy?\nA: Most are. We work with what you have, note what’s missing, and tell you which findings are solid and which are directional. A gap in your data is a finding too.\n\nQ: What does a report look like?\nA: A few slides. The answer is on top, then the evidence, then a short list of what to keep, test and stop. The last slide says what to know before quoting a number: small samples, missing data, anything that limits the call. In a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, that meant marking any content type with fewer than four posts as directional.\n\nQ: Who on our team should be involved?\nA: The person who owns the agency relationships and the budget, usually a brand or marketing lead. During setup, it helps to have someone who can grant access to sales data, email or CRM, and ad accounts. Nobody needs to be technical.\n\nQ: How do agencies react?\nA: It varies. Agencies doing good work tend to welcome a fair count, because it separates their results from claims that didn’t hold up. Pushback is usually about counting rules, and that’s a useful conversation to have once everyone is looking at the same total.\n\nQ: How is this different from a sentiment dashboard?\nA: We count specific things: the words people use, the formats they reward, the complaints that keep coming back. In a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, we counted 6,622 comments and found one word in 62 was slang. A person checks the labels, because sarcasm and in-jokes fool automated sentiment. If you already have a listening tool, we plug it in.\n\nQ: What do you do with what you hear?\nA: Turn it into what to make next. A real Arcus audience report for a streaming service ended with four rules for the brand’s voice, each backed by counts, like replying to fans’ jokes instead of starting your own. Where we can, we check a listening finding against your own numbers before you act on it.\n\n# Guides\n\n## Is your agency double counting your sales?\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/agency-double-counting-sales\nShort answer: If you use more than one agency, probably. Every ad platform credits itself when its ad touched a buyer, so one sale can show up in two or three agency reports. To check, add up every agency’s claims for one month and compare the total with your own numbers for the same month.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nYour agency probably isn’t doing it on purpose. Each agency reports from the platforms it buys on, and each platform gives itself credit whenever its ad was somewhere on the path to a sale. That rule is called attribution, and every platform runs its own version of it.\nPicture one buyer. She sees a creator’s video on Monday. On Thursday she clicks an ad in her feed. On Saturday she types your brand name into a search bar, clicks the top result and buys. The creator agency counts that sale. The social agency counts it. The search agency counts it. Each report is honest about what its platform saw. Put the three reports on one table and you have three sales where there was one. That’s double counting.\nIt gets bigger with two settings most reports never mention. The first is the conversion window: how many days after an ad a sale can still be credited to it. The second is the view-through conversion: a sale credited to an ad someone saw but never clicked. Long windows and view-throughs let every platform reach further back and claim more of the same buyers.\nWhy it matters: budgets, renewals and bonuses are set on these numbers. If three agencies each claim the same buyer, each one’s ROAS looks better than it is, and the total suggests your marketing drove more than it could have. Decisions made on the inflated number tend to put more money behind whichever channel counts most generously. That’s often the one closest to the purchase, like search, which catches people at the last step whether or not anything else brought them there.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample numbers. These figures show how a check reads. They are not from a client.\nA brand works with three agencies. For one quarter, the agencies claim 4,200 results between them. The brand’s own numbers are the starting point: every sale, ticket or sign-up, with a date, a product or title, and the channel it came through. Each claim is matched against that list, and each result can be matched once.\nAgencyClaimedHeld upMain reason the rest fell out\nSocial2,100820Also claimed by another agency\nSearch1,300310People already searching the brand’s name\nCreators800250Codes that spread to coupon sites\nTotal4,2001,380\n\nSo 2,820 claims didn’t hold up. Here is where they went:\n\n- 1,610 were counted twice. Two agencies claimed the same result. Neither gets it until a test says which one earned it.\n- 740 came from the brand’s own emails. The buyer clicked an email and bought. An ad was somewhere in the path, so a platform claimed it.\n- 470 came from people already searching the name. See brand search. Those buyers were on their way.\n\nNobody in this example lied. Three reasonable reports added up to roughly three times what the evidence supports. That gap is what you’re paying against.\n\n## How to check yours in an afternoon\n\n- Pick one month you have every agency’s report for. A month with one big push is best.\n- Write down each agency’s claimed results for that month. Use the number they report to you, the one they put on the slide.\n- Pull your own numbers for the same dates: sales, tickets, sign-ups, streams or viewing, whatever your team counts.\n- Add the claims together and set the total next to your own number.\n- Read the gap. If the claims add up to more than you had in total, some results are being counted more than once. If they add up to most of what you had, ask what happened to email, word of mouth and people who already knew you.\n- Ask each agency two things: what counts as a sale (a click, or a view too) and over how many days.\n\nWant the arithmetic done for you? Paste your numbers into the agency math calculator. Nothing you type leaves your browser.\nWhat the gap tells you, and what it doesn’t: a total above your own number proves overlap. It doesn’t tell you which agency is overcounting, or by how much each. For that you need the detail behind each claim, matched one result at a time against your own list. That’s the slow part, and it’s where most teams stop. It’s also the part that turns an awkward conversation into a useful one, because you can point at specific results instead of a total.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Comparing each agency to your total one at a time. Each one looks fine alone. The problem only shows when you add them up.\n- Mixing date ranges. One agency reports by calendar month, another by flight dates. Line them up first.\n- Forgetting your own channels. Email, your own social accounts and press drive results too. If the agencies’ claims leave no room for them, something is off.\n- Treating the gap as fraud. It almost always comes from platform rules. Lead with curiosity and you’ll get better answers.\n- Cutting the agency with the biggest gap. A big gap can mean generous counting, not weak work. Test before you cut.\n\n## Questions to ask your agencies\n\n- Do you count a sale on a click, a view, or both? Can you split them?\n- What are your click and view windows, in days?\n- How much of what you claimed came from people searching our name?\n- Can you share the dates and IDs behind each claimed result?\n- If we added up every agency’s claims, would you expect the total to match our own numbers?\n\nOur list of 12 questions to ask before you renew goes further, with what a good answer sounds like.\n\n## What this doesn’t prove\n\nMatching claims against your own numbers removes double counting. It tells you which claims are possible. It doesn’t prove an ad caused a sale. Some of the 1,380 results in the example would have happened anyway. For cause, run a holdout test: show the ads in some regions and not others for a few weeks, then compare. Our guide to incrementality walks through it.\nIf you’d rather see it on your own numbers first, bring us one question. “Do our agencies’ claims add up to more than we sold?” is a good one. We check every claim against your own numbers and walk you through what held up in 30 minutes. Free, no card. [Confirm: whether agencies are told about the check.]\n\n## How to read an agency report in 10 minutes\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/read-an-agency-report\nShort answer: Skip the impressions and engagement totals. Find the results the agency claims, how it counted them (clicks or views, and over how many days), what it spent, and how much credit went to people already searching your name. Then compare the claims with your own numbers for the same dates.\n\n## The five numbers that matter\n\nMost agency recaps run 20 to 40 slides. Five numbers in them decide whether the money worked. The rest is context: what ran, where, with which creative. Useful, but it can wait until you know whether the headline claim stands up. Find these first and write them on one page.\nNumberWhy it mattersWhat to ask\nResults claimedSales, tickets, sign-ups or streams: this is what you’re paying forCan you share the dates and IDs behind each claim?\nHow a result was countedCounting views as well as clicks claims far more resultsClicks only, or view-throughs too? Can you split them?\nCounting windowA long window claims results from weeks laterWhat’s the window, for clicks and for views?\nSpendNeeded for any cost per result or ROASDoes it include fees and production?\nBrand search sharePeople searching your name were already comingHow much credit went to brand search?\n\n## The 10-minute routine\n\n- Minutes 1 to 2: find the claim. Skip to the slide with results. Write down the number and the dates it covers.\n- Minutes 3 to 4: find the rule. Look in the footnotes or the appendix for how a result was counted. If you can’t find it, that’s your first question.\n- Minutes 5 to 6: find the spend. Note whether fees are in it.\n- Minutes 7 to 8: put it next to your own numbers. Same dates. Your sales, tickets, sign-ups or viewing. Is the claim a small share of your total, or most of it?\n- Minutes 9 to 10: write three questions. One about the rule, one about the window, one about brand search.\n\nHere’s the one-page sheet to fill in. Keep it, and fill in a new row every month. After a quarter you’ll see each agency’s pattern without opening a deck.\nFieldWhat to write\nAgency and datesWho reported, and the exact dates covered\nResults claimedThe number on the results slide\nCounting ruleClicks only, or clicks and views\nWindowsDays for clicks, days for views\nSpendWith or without fees\nBrand search shareThe share of credit from people searching your name, if they give it\nYour own numberYour sales, tickets, sign-ups or viewing for the same dates\nOpen questionsThe three you’ll ask at the review\n\nWhere to look: the results slide is usually near the front. The counting rules are usually in an appendix, a footnote, or a “methodology” slide near the back. If a recap has no rules anywhere, ask for them in writing before the review, so the meeting is spent on what they mean.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample numbers. These show how a check reads. They are not from a client.\nA search agency’s recap says it drove 1,300 results in a quarter. On its own, that slide looks like a great quarter. Run the routine and three things come up. The counting rule includes people who searched the brand’s name. The brand’s own numbers show other agencies claim some of the same buyers. And when each claim is matched against the brand’s own list, one result at a time, 310 hold up, and most of those 310 came through brand search.\nThat doesn’t mean the agency did bad work. It means the recap answered a different question from the one the brand was asking. The recap answered “what did our platform see?” The brand wanted “what would we lose if we stopped?” Only a test answers that. You can see the full example in our agency check.\nThe same routine works for any agency. A creator agency’s claim depends on whether codes spread to coupon sites. A social agency’s claim depends on view-throughs and the window. A media agency’s claim depends on whether it counted results its partners also reported. Each question takes a minute to ask and saves a quarter of guessing.\n\n## What you can skip\n\nImpressions, reach, engagement rate, “estimated lift” and anything labeled “modeled.” These describe activity. They’re useful to the creative team, and they can tell you whether people watched. They can’t tell you whether anyone bought.\nThere’s one exception. If you want to know which creative worked, views and shares per post are worth keeping, read by what’s on screen. Our guide to telling which posts work shows how.\nBe careful with “engaged” audiences and “attention” scores too. They measure how people interacted with an ad. They don’t tell you whether those people bought, and they can’t tell you whether they would have bought anyway.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Reading the summary slide only. The rules that change the number are in the footnotes.\n- Comparing ROAS across agencies. Two agencies with different windows and view rules are measuring different things. Put them on the same rule first.\n- Reading one agency at a time. The overlap only shows when you add them up. See double counting.\n- Taking “industry benchmark” as a grade. A benchmark tells you how other brands’ reports look. It doesn’t tell you about your results.\n- Asking for more slides. Ask for the detail behind the one slide that matters.\n- Reading the recap in the meeting. Read it the day before. In the room, you want to be asking, not finding.\n- Losing last quarter’s answers. Keep the one-page sheet. Rules quietly change between quarters.\n\n## What this doesn’t prove\n\nTen minutes tells you what to ask. It won’t tell you whether the campaign caused sales. A claim that matches your own numbers is possible, not proven. For proof, you need a holdout test: run the campaign in some regions and not others, and compare.\nIt also won’t tell you whether the agency is good at its job. A report with generous counting can sit on top of strong work, and a careful report can describe a weak quarter. The routine separates the counting from the work, so you can judge each on its own.\nBring your three questions to the next review. If you’d rather have the matching done for you, bring us one question: did this recap hold up? We check it against your own numbers and walk you through what held up in 30 minutes.\n\n## 12 questions to ask your agency before you renew\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/questions-before-you-renew\nShort answer: Ask how they count a sale, over how many days, how much credit goes to brand search and view-throughs, whether their results overlap with your other agencies, and what they would test if the budget were cut. Good agencies answer clearly, share the detail behind each claim, and welcome a test.\n\n## The questions, and what good sounds like\n\n#QuestionA good answerA warning sign\n1How do you count a sale?Clicks only, or clicks and views reported separately“The platform handles that”\n2Over how many days?A short window, and results at a shorter one on requestNobody knows the window\n3How much of the credit is view-through?A numberA shrug, or “it’s standard”\n4How much went to people searching our name?A number, and a plan to test it. See brand searchBrand search folded into the total\n5Can you share the detail behind each claim?Yes, with datesOnly a summary slide\n6Do your results overlap with our other agencies?“Let’s check together”“Ours are separate”\n7Which creator codes showed up on coupon sites?A list. See creator codesNever looked\n8What did you test this quarter, and what did you stop?Something specific they stoppedEverything is working\n9What would you cut first if the budget dropped 20%?A clear first cut“Everything is essential”\n10Which result are you least sure about?An honest pickNone\n11Would you run a holdout test before we increase spend?Yes, with a design. See holdout testReasons it can’t be done\n12Who on your team reads our data, and how often?A name and a cadence“The platform reports it”\n\nQuestions 1 to 4 are about the counting rules. Questions 5 to 7 are about overlap. Questions 8 to 12 tell you how the agency thinks. You need all three.\nA note on tone. These questions work best framed as housekeeping: you’re putting every agency on the same rules so you can compare them fairly. That’s true, and it’s easier for an agency to answer than a question that sounds like an audit. Agencies that do good work usually like this framing, because fair rules make their results easier to defend inside your company.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample numbers. These show how a renewal conversation can go. They are not from a client.\nA creator agency reports 800 results for the quarter and asks for a bigger budget. The brand asks question 7. The agency hasn’t checked coupon sites. When the codes are matched against the brand’s own numbers, many of the uses come from coupon sites, where people who were already buying pick up a discount on the way. After matching, 250 of the 800 hold up.\nThe brand still renews. The creators who drove the 250 are clearly working. What changes is the brief: new codes for each flight, a list of the creators whose codes held up, and a holdout test before the budget grows. The answers turned a yes-or-no renewal into a better contract.\nNotice what didn’t happen. Nobody accused the agency of anything. The 550 results that fell out were mostly coupon-site uses, which the platform counted by its own rules. The conversation was about the rule, and the agency left with a clearer brief than it came in with.\n\n## If you only have time for three\n\nSome renewals happen fast. If you get one meeting and ten minutes, ask these three, in this order.\n\n- Can you share the detail behind each claim, with dates? This one opens up everything else. If the answer is yes, you can check the rest yourself, or have it checked.\n- How much of the credit went to people searching our name, and to view-throughs? These are the two places counting gets most generous. A clear number for each tells you how much of the total to look at twice.\n- Would you run a holdout test before we increase spend? A confident agency says yes and starts sketching regions. That answer tells you more about how they think than any case study.\n\nWrite down the answers word for word. Vague answers read very differently a week later on paper.\nIf you can add a fourth, ask question 6, about overlap with your other agencies. It’s the one that explains why claims add up to more than you had. Our guide to double counting shows the arithmetic.\n\n## How to run the meeting\n\n- Send the questions a week ahead. You want considered answers, and a good agency will appreciate the notice.\n- Ask the same questions of every agency. Different counting rules are the most common reason claims add up to more than you sold.\n- Write the answers in one table, one column per agency. The differences jump out.\n- Agree on one follow-up. Usually the detail behind one claim, or a test design.\n- Decide after the follow-up arrives, not in the room.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Asking only at renewal. By then the decision feels urgent. Ask quarterly and renewals get easy.\n- Accepting “industry standard.” A standard window is still a window. Ask what it is.\n- Treating every warning sign as a reason to leave. One weak answer is a conversation. A pattern of them is a decision.\n- Grading on creative alone. Great work and generous counting can live in the same agency. Judge each separately.\n- Forgetting to ask what they’re proud of. The answer tells you what they’ll fight to keep.\n- Letting the agency pick the month. Choose the period yourself, ideally one with a big push, so the answers cover the work that mattered.\n\n## What the answers don’t prove\n\nGood answers mean an agency counts carefully. They don’t mean its work caused your results. Clear counting rules remove double counting. Only a test shows cause. If an agency’s answers are strong and you’re about to give it more money, that’s the moment to run a holdout test, so the bigger budget is backed by proof.\nAnd a strong agency on paper can still be the wrong fit for where you’re going. If your next year is a retail launch, a new market or a new audience, ask what the agency has done there. The answers above tell you how it counts. They don’t tell you whether it can do the next job.\nIf you want the numbers checked before you sign, bring us one question: did this agency’s results hold up? You’ll get a few slides on what held up and a walkthrough you can take into the renewal.\n\n## What is first-party data, and what do I do with mine?\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/first-party-data-for-brand-marketers\nShort answer: First-party data is what your company collects itself: sales, tickets, sign-ups, viewing, customers, email, your own social accounts and site visits. It tells you which posts, campaigns and agencies line up with what actually happened, without relying on what platforms or agencies say. Start with one question and the data you already hold that touches it.\n\n## What counts\n\nFirst-party data is anything your company collects directly. Third-party data comes from outside: public conversation, search trends, reviews and research. Most brand teams have more of the first kind than they think. It’s just in pieces: sales with finance, email with the CRM team, social stats with an agency, each in a tool that doesn’t talk to the others.\nA useful test: if your company could hand the data to an auditor and say “we recorded this ourselves,” it’s first-party. If you’d have to say “a platform told us” or “a vendor estimated it,” treat it with more care. Agency reports sit in between. You received them directly, but they describe what the platforms saw, which is why they’re worth checking against the rest.\nDataWhat it can tell youWhere it usually lives\nSales, tickets, sign-ups, viewingWhat happened, when, and through which channelFinance, ticketing, retail partner reports, app or platform dashboards\nCustomersWho buys again, and who your best customers areYour CRM or customer list\nEmailWhich messages drive results, and how many results came from emailYour email tool\nYour social accountsWhich posts get watched and which get sharedPlatform exports or your social tool\nAd accountsWhat ran, when, where, and what it costThe ad platforms, often run by agencies\nAgency reportsWhat each agency says it droveDecks and spreadsheets in your inbox\n\n## Questions it can answer\n\n- Which posts work? In a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, the brand’s own social export showed one type of post, a trick with the can you could copy tonight, got a median of 19,188 views on Instagram against an account average of 8,148. That’s 2.4 times the average, read from data the brand already had. See the content report.\n- Did the campaign line up with results? Your own numbers are the ground truth agency reports should be checked against.\n- How much came from email? Email results are often claimed by an ad platform that was somewhere in the path. Your email tool knows better.\n- Who are our best customers? Usually a small group drives a big share of results. Knowing who they are changes who you write for.\n\nNotice what these questions have in common. Each one is about a decision: what to make, what to fund, who to renew, who to talk to. First-party data earns its keep when it’s pointed at a decision someone has to make this quarter. Pointed at nothing in particular, it turns into a dashboard nobody opens.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample numbers. These show how first-party data checks a claim. They are not from a client.\nA brand’s agencies claim 4,200 results in a quarter. The brand matches every claim against its own list of sales, one result at a time. 1,380 hold up. Of the 2,820 that don’t, 740 trace back to the brand’s own emails: the buyer clicked an email and bought, and an ad platform claimed the sale because the buyer had seen an ad that week. Without the email data, nobody could have told.\nThat’s the job first-party data does best. It isn’t glamorous. It’s the list of what happened, which every other number should agree with.\n\n## Better together\n\nOn its own, first-party data tells you what your customers did. Third-party data tells you what people are saying. Lined up, they tell you whether the conversation turns into results.\nIn a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, only 1.9% of 4,283 questions men asked each other on one large forum were about hobbies, games and entertainment. They didn’t ask each other what to watch. The talk happened under the shows instead: 78,682 comments under four trailers. That changes where a brand should listen, and which of its own numbers, like viewing by title, it should line the conversation up against. See the audience report and our guide to listening that counts.\nThe same pattern works for a drinks brand, a game or a fashion label. Count what people say about the product, the moment or the occasion, then line it up week by week with your own sales, sign-ups or streams. When a topic rises in conversation and your numbers rise with it, that’s worth testing. When it rises and your numbers don’t move, that’s worth knowing too.\n\n## Where to start: a checklist\n\n- Write down one question you’ve been asked and couldn’t answer. “Is the creator program working?” is a good one.\n- List the data that touches it: sales or sign-ups by day, creator codes, the creator agency’s report, your email sends.\n- Find who owns each piece. Finance, a partner, an agency, your own team.\n- Check the dates line up. Same days, same time zone, same definition of a result.\n- Put the agency’s claim next to your own number for the same dates.\n- Write down what you still can’t answer. That list is your next step.\n\n## Common mistakes and limits\n\n- Waiting until it’s all in one place. Start with the two or three sources one question needs.\n- Letting the ad platforms be the record. They report what they saw. Your own numbers report what happened.\n- Collecting more instead of reading what you have. Most teams already hold data nobody reads.\n- Forgetting retail and partners. If most of your results come through someone else, their reports are part of your first-party picture.\n\nQuestions to ask your agencies: Can you share results by day so we can line them up with our own numbers? Which of our own channels, like email, could have driven the results you’re claiming? What do you need from our data to report more accurately?\nWhat it doesn’t prove: first-party data tells you what happened and lets you check claims. On its own it can’t tell you why. A spike in sign-ups the week a campaign ran lines up with the campaign. Proving it caused them takes a holdout test.\nIf nobody on your team has time for the checklist, bring us one question, the one from step 1. We answer it with the data that touches it and walk you through it in 30 minutes, so you see what your data can already tell you. After that, Arcus connects every piece into one layer you own, inside the tools you already use, adds what people say online, and answers the next question with the whole picture.\n\n## Incrementality, explained without the math\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/incrementality-without-the-math\nShort answer: Incrementality means the results that happened because of a campaign and wouldn’t have happened without it. The clearest way to measure it is a holdout test: run the campaign in some regions and not others for a few weeks, then compare. The difference is the campaign’s effect.\n\n## What it means\n\nEvery campaign gets credit for results. Some of those results would have happened anyway. The people who already loved you, the fans who were going to stream the new season, the buyers searching your name: they show up in the campaign report, but the campaign didn’t bring them. Incrementality is what’s left when you take them out.\nThe two-sentence version for your boss: we ran the campaign in some regions and not in others. The difference in results between the two groups is what the campaign did.\nIt works the same whatever you count. For a studio, the result might be tickets in each region. For a streaming service, sign-ups or hours viewed. For a drinks brand, sales through retail partners by region. As long as you can see the result by region, or by some other group you can split, you can run the test.\n\n## Why matching isn’t enough\n\nMatching every agency claim to a result in your own numbers removes double counting. That’s a big step. It tells you which claims are possible. It can’t tell you which ones the campaign caused.\nExample numbers. In our example agency check, 470 claimed results came from people already searching the brand’s name. Those buyers clicked an ad on their way to a purchase they’d already decided on. Matching catches the obvious cases like that. It can’t catch the quieter ones: the fan who saw a trailer and would have watched anyway, or the regular who bought her usual case the week your ad ran. Only a comparison against people who didn’t see the campaign can.\nThis matters most for the channels that sit closest to the purchase. Brand search, retargeting and last-click credit all catch people at the final step. Their claimed results can look excellent while adding little, because the buyers were already on their way. Those are usually the first channels worth testing.\n\n## The test, step by step\n\n- Write the question. “Does paid social drive sign-ups in our top markets?” One channel, one result.\n- Pick regions that look alike: similar size, similar history of sales, tickets or sign-ups.\n- Split them into two groups. Run the campaign in one. Leave the other alone. This is the holdout.\n- Agree the length before you start, long enough for results to move, usually a few weeks.\n- Change nothing else. No new promotions in one group only, no price changes, no surprise press push.\n- Compare results between the two groups using your own numbers, not the platform’s.\n- Write down what you’ll do with each outcome before you look.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample. This shows how a test reads. It is not from a client.\nA brand’s search agency claims a large share of results, and most of what holds up after matching came through brand search. The question: if we stopped paying for clicks on our own name, would those buyers still find us? The test pauses brand search in half the brand’s regions for three weeks and keeps it on in the other half.\n\n- If results hold in the paused regions, people were finding the brand without the paid click. That budget can move somewhere that earns it.\n- If results drop, the channel works, and the size of the drop tells you by how much.\n\nEither way the brand learns something it couldn’t learn from any report. And the test itself is cheap compared with a year of spending on a guess: three weeks, half the regions, and a comparison the whole team can read. You can see this example in our agency check.\n\n## When it’s worth running: a checklist\n\n- You’re about to move a meaningful amount of budget.\n- You’re about to renew an agency on the strength of its claims.\n- A channel’s numbers look too good, like brand search or view-throughs.\n- Two agencies claim the same results and you need to know which one earned them.\n- You can hold out regions without hurting a launch you can’t repeat.\n\nHow long and how big: long enough for results to move and settle, and big enough that normal week-to-week swings don’t drown out the difference. For most brand campaigns that means a few weeks and a meaningful share of your regions. If your results are small or noisy, run longer rather than smaller. Agree all of this before the test starts, in writing, with the agency.\nWho should run it? The agency can set up the regions, since it controls the buying. Reading the result should start from your own numbers, and ideally be done by someone with no stake in the answer. That keeps the conversation afterward about what the numbers say.\nWrite the plan on one page: the question, the regions in each group, the dates, the result you’ll compare, and what you’ll do if results hold, rise or drop. Share it with everyone before the test starts.\nQuestions to ask your agency: Would you run a holdout before we increase spend? Which regions would you hold out, and for how long? What result would make you recommend cutting the channel?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what a test doesn’t prove\n\n- Stopping too early. Results take time to move. Agree the length up front and keep to it.\n- Choosing regions that aren’t alike. A big city against small towns tells you about geography.\n- Changing other things mid-test. One extra promotion in one group spoils the comparison.\n- Reading the platform’s numbers. Use your own. The platform can’t see the regions where it didn’t run.\n- Testing during your one big launch. Pick a period you can repeat.\n\nA test measures one campaign, in one set of regions, over one period. It doesn’t tell you what the campaign will do at twice the budget, in a different season, or with new creative. It also doesn’t replace a media mix model, which estimates every channel at once from history. A test measures one channel directly, and it’s the best way to check what a model says.\nWe design and read these tests as part of Arcus, on your own numbers. To start smaller, bring us one question, like which of your agencies’ claims hold up before you test anything. If the answer points to a channel worth testing, we’ll say so.\n\n## Social listening that counts, not guesses\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/social-listening-that-counts\nShort answer: Useful listening counts specific things in your audience’s own words: what they post, what they reward, which words they use, and what they never mention. Then it checks those patterns against your own numbers. Sentiment scores and word clouds rarely change a decision. Counts and real quotes do.\n\n## Listen where they talk\n\nYour audience doesn’t talk in one place, and it rarely talks where your brand posts. Fans talk under trailers on YouTube, in Discord servers, in Twitch and Kick chat, in Reddit threads, in TikTok comments, in Facebook groups, and in reviews on places like Goodreads, Letterboxd and Steam. Search data shows what they look for. The full list of where we read is on our listening page.\nWhereWhat you learn there\nComments under trailers and postsWhat fans reward: the callback, the joke, the verdict\nReddit and forumsWhat people ask each other, and what they never raise\nTikTok formats they run themselvesThe bits and the topics they own\nDiscord, Twitch and Kick chatLive reaction, in their own words\nReviewsWhat the product is judged on\nSearchWhat they look for, and when\n\nThe rooms matter more than the mentions. In a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, men 18 to 24 almost never asked each other what to watch. Of 4,283 questions on one large men’s forum, 1.9% were about hobbies, games and entertainment. Entertainment came up as a question in 47 titles, with a median of one upvote. The talk happened under the shows themselves: 78,682 comments under four of the service’s trailers on YouTube. A tool that tracked brand mentions would have found a quiet audience. Reading the right rooms found a loud one. See the audience report.\n\n## Count what they do\n\nA summary says “young men use a lot of slang.” A count says one word in every 62 is slang. The first leads a brand to write more slang. The second leads it to write less.\nCounting also lets you compare. If you count your audience and your brand with the same script, the gap between them is a finding. If you count the same rooms every quarter, a change is a finding. A summary can’t do either, because two summaries written by different people can’t be lined up.\nHere’s what counting looked like in that report:\n\n- 293 TikToks from eight formats the audience runs themselves, each sorted by hand.\n- 2,965 comments measured for length, first person, punctuation and hashtags.\n- 480 comments labeled by hand, one kind each: a joke, a verdict, a question, a sincere line.\n- 6,622 comments counted for the words they use.\n\nThe counts changed the voice rules. An acted-out sketch got 42 times the views of a sincere post. The most searched slang of 2025, “6-7” at 141,000 searches, appeared in one comment of the 6,622. A sentiment score wouldn’t have shown either.\n\n## A worked example: the slang question\n\nThe brand’s question was simple: should our captions sound like our audience? The listening answered it in three counts.\nWhat was countedThe audienceThe brand’s captions\nWords in a typical line58\nLines ending in a full stop4.9%35%\nLines with a hashtag0.2%92.5%\n\nThe words they did use were verdicts on the work, like “peak,” “real” and “cinema.” “Rizz” appeared in none. The answer: the brand’s captions already sounded like a brand, and adding slang would make it worse. Write shorter, drop the hashtags, and let fans supply the words. Our guide to how young audiences write has the full count.\nNotice the shape of the answer. It isn’t “young men are sarcastic” or “use more humor.” It’s three measurable differences between the audience and the brand, each one something a social team can change in its next caption. That’s the test for any listening finding: could someone act on it this week?\n\n## A listening brief you can use tomorrow\n\n- Write the decision the listening should inform. “Should we change our caption voice?” beats “what are people saying?”\n- Name the rooms. Where does this audience talk when you’re not posting?\n- Name what you’ll count. Views by format, likes on the top comment, words per 1,000 comments, topics per 100 questions.\n- Count the brand the same way. Same script, same measures, your own posts.\n- Keep real quotes with their like counts, so every claim has an example.\n- Line it up with your own numbers. Do the topics that spike in conversation show up in sales, sign-ups or viewing?\n- End with keep, test, stop.\n\nQuestions to ask your listening agency or vendor: Which rooms do you read beyond brand mentions? What do you count, and can we see the counts? Do you measure our own posts with the same rules? Can you show three real quotes, with like counts, for each finding? What did a client change because of your last report?\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Tracking mentions only. Your audience may never say your name. They talk about the show, the drink, the moment.\n- Trusting sentiment scores. A top reply under a brand post called the trailer bad and promised to watch anyway. It’s sarcasm, and it means “I’m in.” A sentiment tool would score it negative.\n- Reading only the top posts. The comments are often where the meaning is.\n- Stopping at a word cloud. If a listening report ends with one, it isn’t done.\n- Counting the brand differently. If the audience is counted by script and the brand is judged by eye, the comparison doesn’t hold.\n- Quoting without counts. One vivid comment can mislead. Pair every quote with how often its kind shows up.\n\n## What it doesn’t prove\n\nListening tells you what people say and reward in public. It doesn’t tell you who buys. Public rooms skew: the forum in the example skews older than 24, so the report showed the 57 posters who stated an age of 18 to 24 separately. Some platforms hide counts: Instagram returns no like counts on comments. Lining it up with your own numbers can be as simple as a weekly table: the count of a topic in conversation next to sign-ups, sales or viewing by title for the same weeks. When the two move together more than once, you have something worth testing. Listening gets useful when you line it up with your first-party data, and it becomes proof only with a test.\nArcus plugs in the listening tool you already have, reads the rooms your audience uses, and lines what people say up with your own numbers. To see it on your audience, bring us one question, like “Should our captions sound like our fans?”\n\n## How to tell which of your posts work\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/which-posts-actually-work\nShort answer: Group your posts by what happens on screen, then compare each group’s median views and shares with your account average. Watched and shared are different jobs, so check both. Mark groups of fewer than four posts as directional, and end with what to keep, test and stop.\n\n## Sort by what’s on screen\n\nCampaign names describe intentions. What’s on screen describes what people saw. Watch every post and give it one label for the thing that happens: a trick with the product, a joke about friends, the product as the character, a scene where nothing happens. If a post doesn’t fit, put it in “other.” You’ll come back to that bucket.\nA few rules make the labels hold up. Label the thing that happens, not the mood or the message. One label per post. If two people would label a post differently, write down why and pick one. Watch with the sound off once: if the joke still lands, note it. In the report below, the most shared posts read with the sound off.\nIn a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, every Instagram and Facebook post from one summer campaign was watched and sorted this way: 39 Instagram posts and 29 Facebook posts. See the content report.\n\n## Use medians, compared with the account average\n\nThe median is the middle post in a group. It keeps one viral hit from hiding a weak pattern. Divide it by the account’s average per post and you get a number anyone can read: above 1 beats the account, below 1 doesn’t. Here is the Instagram scorecard from that report:\nWhat’s on screenPostsMedian viewsvs. averageMedian shares\nHosting hacks: a trick with the can419,1882.36x26\nA moment people were in*38,0000.98x46\nOther76,1860.76x14\nCan tricks and gags116,1070.75x13\nWorkday escape73,9620.49x10\nUnusual hangouts52,4150.30x12\n3D animation*21,1390.14x8\n\nAccount average: 8,148 views and 36 shares per post. *Fewer than four posts, so directional.\n\n## Check for one-post groups\n\nMedians help, but also ask how much of a group’s total comes from its best post. In the same report, one post, a sign held up from a balcony, carried 45% of its group’s views and 77% of its shares. Without it, the group’s median fell from 3,962 to 3,119 views, and the group was the second weakest on the account. The hit was a permission gag with a sight joke. Two anti-work posts in the group, played straight, stayed under 2,300 views each. One post had been carrying an idea that didn’t work.\nNow check the other direction: posts in “other” that land together. In the report, three posts about the end of a big summer tournament weren’t in any named group. Together they were number one by shares and number two by views on Instagram. The brief said to name a group when two or more posts land together, so the report named it a candidate, marked it directional, and set a test: does the next big shared moment do the same?\n\n## Watched and shared are different jobs\n\nHosting hacks led on views but sat below average on shares: a median of 26 against the account’s 36. The most shared post on the account wasn’t in any named group. It was a joke about the viewer’s friends going back to work after a big tournament: 409 shares on 8,000 views, 11 times the Instagram average for shares. Facebook was different again: it watched, and barely shared, at 1.2 shares per post. Our guides to watched versus shared and what to post on Facebook go deeper.\nSo build two scorecards, one for views and one for shares, per platform. A group can win one and lose the other, and that’s useful. It tells you which job each kind of post does, so you can plan a mix instead of chasing one number.\n\n## A checklist for your next review\n\n- Export every post from the period, with views and shares, from your own accounts.\n- Watch each one. Give it one label for what happens on screen.\n- Work out the account average per post for views and shares, per platform. Never add platforms together.\n- Find each group’s median and divide by the average.\n- Mark groups of fewer than four posts as directional.\n- For each group, check the best post’s share of the total. If one post carries most of it, read the group without it.\n- Look in “other” for two or more posts that land together. Name them as a candidate group.\n- Write three lines: keep, test, stop.\n\nHere are the three lines from the report, as an example of how specific they should be. Keep hosting hacks: more physical tricks with the can that someone could copy tonight. Test a friend-group frame on top of the formats that already get watched, and watch the share rate. Retire 3D animation, and the workday idea as written: keep the permission gag, drop “quit your job.”\nQuestions to ask your social agency: How do you group posts, by brief or by what’s on screen? Which group would you stop? Which hit is carrying a weak group?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Ranking by totals. A group with more posts wins by volume.\n- Reading share rate alone. Low-view posts can post high rates on a handful of shares. The 3D animation group had the highest median share rate on Instagram, 0.9%, and the lowest views.\n- Calling a group on two posts. Mark it directional and test again.\n- Sorting by campaign name. Two posts from the same brief can do opposite things on screen. Label what happens.\n- Ignoring “other.” In the report, “other” had higher median views than three of the four named groups, and the most shared post on the account came out of it.\n- Forgetting test posts. In the report, one post was a test cut at 212 views. It stayed in the data, labeled, and wasn’t read as a creative failure.\n\nThis tells you which kinds of posts get watched and shared. It doesn’t tell you which ones sold. Lining posts up with your own sales, tickets or sign-ups is the next step, and a test is the step after that. Arcus does both once your social accounts and your sales sit in one layer. To start, bring us one question, like “Which of our posts moved sales?”\n\n## Do you need a data team? A checklist\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/do-you-need-a-data-team\nShort answer: You need a dedicated data team if data is core to how your company competes and you have new questions every week. If you mainly need trustworthy answers about agencies, content and your audience a few times a month, a setup built inside the tools you already use, and owned by you, is usually faster than hiring and cheaper than a team.\n\n## The three paths\n\nBrand teams that want better answers usually pick one of three paths.\nHire a data teamBuy analytics softwareHave it built in your own tools\nWho does the workYour hiresWhoever on your team has timeAn outside team\nTime to first answerMonths to hire, then months to buildWeeks to buy, then setup by your teamA first answer early, full setup in about three months\nWhat you managePeople, tools and the setupThe tool and the setupThe questions. The setup is yours.\nBest forData is core to how you competeYou already have analystsYou have questions, not analysts\n\nOur comparisons go deeper on hiring a team and analytics software.\nWhat a marketing data team needs, at minimum: someone to connect the data from agencies, partners and platforms; someone to analyze it and design tests; someone to read public conversation if that matters to you; and someone senior enough to turn findings into decisions. In a small team those can be two people. Rarely one.\nIt also needs time. Connecting sources, agreeing definitions and checking the first numbers takes months before anyone answers the question you hired them for.\n\n## The checklist\n\n- How often do you have questions? Every week, or a few a month?\n- Can you hire and keep analysts and data engineers in your market, at your pay range?\n- Who will manage them? Someone who can judge their work, not only their output.\n- Is your data already in one place, or spread across agencies, partners and tools?\n- Do you need answers this quarter?\n- Does anyone on the team read dashboards today, or do they go unread?\n- Do you need to read conversation online as well as your own data?\n- Would a few slides with the answer on top be enough for your leadership?\n- Do your questions change with each launch, season or release?\n- Is checking agencies a big part of the job? An in-house team can find that awkward when the agencies are long-time partners.\n\n## How to read your answers\n\nMostly “weekly, yes, we have someone, it’s already in one place”: build the team. You’ll get value from people who live in your data every day.\nMostly “a few a month, not sure, no, it’s spread out”: get help first. You’ll get answers sooner, and you’ll learn which questions matter before you hire anyone to answer them.\nMixed answers are common. Some teams get help now and hire later, once the setup is running and they know what kind of person they need.\nTwo answers carry extra weight. If you need answers this quarter, hiring is unlikely to deliver them in time. And if checking agencies is a big part of the job, an outside team can do it without the relationships getting in the way.\n\n## A worked example\n\nExample. A composite to show how the checklist reads, not a client.\nA brand marketing lead at a beverage brand works with three agencies and a creator program. Her questions come in bursts: before each renewal, after each campaign, when the CMO asks whether summer worked. Her sales data sits with finance and retail partners. Social exports sit with the agencies. Nobody on her team writes queries, and she doesn’t want to learn.\nHer checklist reads: a few a month, hard to hire, nobody to manage them, spread out, needed this quarter, dashboards unread, yes to listening, yes to a few slides, questions change every season, checking agencies is central. That’s a clear “get help first.” Hiring would take months before her first answer, and her first hire would spend their first months connecting data before answering anything.\nWhat she does instead: she brings one question, whether her agencies’ summer claims held up against her sales data, sees the answer, and takes it into her next renewal. If that first answer earns its place, the setup follows. A few months later she has the answers she was missing, and a much better idea of whether she’ll ever need to hire.\n\n## What getting help looks like with Arcus\n\nArcus is the third path for entertainment and lifestyle brands. The Arcus team connects every piece of your data, from sales and email to social accounts and agency reports, into one layer you own, inside the tools you already use, and adds what people say online. It takes about three months, and there’s no new software for your team to learn. Answers arrive as a few slides: the answer on top, the evidence, then what to keep, test and stop. [Confirm: read-only access to connected accounts, and where data is stored.]\nOnce it’s connected, any leader can ask a question and get an answer that sees the whole picture: whether an agency’s claims held up, which posts work, who a launch is really for. See how it works.\nThe way in is small on purpose. Your first question is free, with a 30-minute walkthrough. After that there’s a one-time setup fee of [$X] and a monthly fee of [$Y]. The pricing page has the details.\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this checklist doesn’t decide\n\n- Hiring one analyst and expecting a team. One person ends up connecting data instead of answering questions.\n- Buying software nobody has time to set up. A tool doesn’t answer questions on its own.\n- Waiting for perfect data. Start with one question and the data that touches it.\n- Hiring before you know the questions. The questions decide the skills. Write ten of them down before you write a job description.\n- Treating it as forever. The right answer this year may change next year.\n\nQuestions to ask anyone offering to do it for you: Who owns the setup, and where does it live? Which of our existing tools do you connect to? Who reads the data, and how often? What do we get each month, and in what form? What happens to the setup if we stop? [Confirm: the answers Arcus gives to each.]\nThe checklist tells you which path fits your questions today. It doesn’t tell you what any path will cost your company, or how good your data is until someone looks. The quickest way to see what your data can answer is to bring us one question. We answer it with your own numbers and walk you through it in 30 minutes.\n\n## What a 42x view gap says about your brand voice\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/what-a-42x-view-gap-says-about-brand-voice\nShort answer: It says the audience rewards a joke it can join, so a brand voice should play along with their bits rather than preach or start its own. In a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, an acted-out bit had a median of 91,850 views against 2,171 for a sincere post and 1,675 for a brand post. Viewers liked the sincere posts at the same rate per view; they reached 42 times fewer people.\n\n## The numbers\n\nA streaming service wanted to know how to keep young men around between seasons. Arcus read men 18 to 24 in their own posts and comments on TikTok, Reddit, YouTube, X and Instagram, and counted what they did. On TikTok, that meant 293 posts from eight formats the audience runs themselves, like the boys’ group chat, the gym, the fantasy league and the boys’ trip. Each post was watched and sorted by hand. Three kinds matter here.\nThe kinds are simple. A bit is acted out and has a punchline. A sincere post is someone talking straight to camera about advice, motivation or a feeling. A brand post is a company account trying the same register; one in the set, a post selling an app, did 2,242 views, close to the brand median.\nKind of postWhat it isPostsMedian viewsLikes on the top comment (median)\nThe bitA sketch with a punchline, acted out3091,850242\nThe sincere postAdvice, motivation or a feeling, said straight to camera682,1713\nThe brand postA company account trying the same register251,6751\n\n91,850 divided by 2,171 is 42. That’s the gap. Against a brand post it’s 55. The order held in seven of the eight formats. The biggest bit in the set, the boys’ group chat played as a hostage scene, did 22.5 million views. See the audience report.\n\n## Same like rate, 42 times the reach\n\nHere’s the part most teams miss. Per viewer, a sincere post is liked as often as a bit: 107 likes per 1,000 views for sincere, 106 for the bit. The people who saw the sincere posts liked them. Far fewer people saw them.\nSo viewers didn’t rate the sincere posts lower. The difference is how many people each kind reached. And under a bit, the comments become a second act. A quarter of the most-liked comments across the study played along with a bit: extending it, quoting it back, or pitching it as a film. The most-liked of those, under a group-chat sketch, read “could be a amazing movie if it was a real movie” and took 464,928 likes.\nThe top comment under a bit carried a median of 242 likes. Under a sincere post, 3. Under a brand post, 1. When a brand posts, the room has little to join.\n\n## The sincere post that traveled\n\nOne sincere post in the 293 broke out. A bricklayer posted a clip of his 19-year-old apprentice asleep after a 90-day outage: 5,007,997 views. Nothing about it was a bit. What it had was a real person in the frame, someone the viewer could recognize.\nThat’s the useful exception. Sincere content can work for this audience when someone real is on screen and the brand isn’t the one talking. It doesn’t work as a caption from a company account telling people how to feel.\n\n## What it means for your brand voice\n\nThe report turned the counts into four rules. None of them asks a brand to be funnier than its audience.\n\n- Play along. Reply to the bit. Don’t start one. Be the callback, the riff, the pitch. A line the report offered as a model: “rewatch starts tonight. season 1, episode 1. the group chat has been warned.”\n- Tell, don’t ask. State a time and when it comes back. Don’t ask the room a question. Questions were 3.9% of the most-liked comments. The model is a line from one of their own threads, 44 points: “It should not even be a question. We tee off at X o’clock.”\n- Hand over the mic. Let a fan’s line be the caption. One of the service’s regional accounts posted a short line in the fans’ own voice for a men’s title and did 12.1 million views. The main US account’s biggest men’s post did 524,100.\n- No glossary. No slang in the caption. One word in 62 they write is slang, and they use those words to judge things. See how young audiences write.\n\nThe voice already existed inside the company, on its X account and its regional TikTok accounts. A short, flat line from the X account naming a season and episode, with a two-word sign-off, took more than 1,500 likes. The job was to move that voice to the main account. Most brands are in the same spot: someone on the team already writes like this.\nThere’s one more signal worth knowing. The audience grades the marketing. A comment giving the marketing team advice on how to bill the new season took 54,000 likes under one of the service’s trailers. When the marketing talked like a fan, the replies said so. They notice either way.\n\n## Run it on your own account\n\n- Pull your last 30 posts on one platform and the views for each.\n- Label each one: a bit, a sincere post, a product post, other. Watch them; don’t guess from the caption.\n- Find the median views for each label. Divide the biggest by the smallest.\n- Read the top comment under each post and note its likes. Where is the room joining in?\n- Find the accounts your audience already follows for the bit you want. Could you reply there instead of posting?\n- Rewrite your next five captions with the four rules. No questions to the room. No slang. A time, if there is one.\n- Compare medians after a month.\n\nQuestions to ask your social agency: Which of our posts would you call a bit, and how did they do against the rest? Who on our side already writes in the audience’s voice? What would you reply to this week instead of posting?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Making the brand the comedian. Brand posts did worst of the three kinds. The rules are about joining, not performing.\n- Writing off sincerity. The like rate was the same. Put a real person in the frame.\n- Copying the format without the room. A bit works because the audience already runs it. Find theirs first.\n- Reading averages. One 22.5 million view post would swamp an average. Medians keep the comparison fair.\n\nThe counts come from one audience, men 18 to 24, on public TikTok posts in eight formats, sorted by hand in September 2026. They show what gets watched and joined. They don’t show that a bit will move sign-ups or viewing for your brand. That needs your own numbers lined up against the posts, and a test. Listening that counts is covered in this guide.\nTo run this count on your own audience, bring us one question, like “What does our audience reward that we never post?” We line up what people say online with your own numbers and walk you through the answer in 30 minutes.\n\n## How young audiences write, counted\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/how-young-audiences-write\nShort answer: Short, first person and almost never with a hashtag. In a real Arcus audience report, men 18 to 24 wrote five-word lines, used one slang word in every 62, and put a hashtag in 0.2% of comments, while the brand’s own captions carried one 92.5% of the time. Write shorter, drop the hashtags, and leave the slang to them.\n\n## How they write\n\nFor a streaming service, Arcus measured 2,965 comments in the TikTok formats men 18 to 24 run themselves, then measured the brand’s own TikTok captions from the same month with the same script. Same rules, both sides.\nMeasureTheir commentsThe brand’s captions\nWords in a typical line58\nEnds in a full stop4.9%35%\nSays I, me or we37%5%\nCarries a hashtag0.2%92.5%\nAll lower case27.6%Not measured\nSwears3.4%Not measured\n\nOne of the service’s competitors already captions at the audience’s rate. The brand’s typical caption was eight words, a full stop and a show hashtag. The audience noticed. Under one of the brand’s posts, a reply asked, less politely, who wrote the caption. They can hear a script in a few words. See the audience report.\n\n## What they reward\n\nArcus labeled the three most-liked comments under every post in ten TikTok pulls, 360 comments in all, with one kind each.\nKind of commentShare of the 360A real example, with likes\nPlays along with the bit25%“could be a amazing movie if it was a real movie” (464,928)\nA flat verdict13.9%“Tuff and cringe at the same time” (86,630)\nRoast or banter10.8%“What he be sounding like” (53,948)\nJoke at own expense10.6%Under a friends’ trip post (21,418)\nSincere10.3%“That ain’t a lazy sleep that’s an exhausted sleep” (143,571)\nHype, superlative praise8.1%“you got them mad, i like you” (227,672)\nA question3.9%\nPlain reaction3.9%\nSarcasm, deadpan2.8%A reply calling it bad and promising to watch anyway\nA demand on the poster2.5%\n\nJokes of some kind make up 49%. Sincere lines are about one in ten of the top comments, though they’re 14% of a random sample: they write sincerely more often than the room rewards it. Sarcasm is one comment in 36, but when it lands it’s the second most-liked kind, with a median of 9,796 likes. It’s aimed at the post, never at other commenters. Questions are rare. They don’t ask the room.\nWhat they talk about matters as much as how. Across 4,283 questions on one large men’s forum, 36.7% were about dating and relationships. Among the 57 posters who stated an age of 18 to 24, it was 36.8%. Hobbies, games and entertainment made up 1.9%. They don’t ask each other what to watch. They talk under the show itself: 78,682 comments under four of the service’s trailers on YouTube. A brand that wants to join the conversation should go to that thread, not start a new one.\n\n## Their words, counted\n\nAcross 6,622 comments from their rooms, Arcus counted each term once per comment.\nThe words they usePer 1,000 commentsThe glossary wordsPer 1,000 comments\n😭 (crying-laughing)63unc0.8\nbro40sigma0.6\npeak8.3mog0.6\nreal7.9skibidi0.2\naura5.7670.2\nlock in5.7rizz0\nfr4.1huzz0\ncinema1.7gyat0\n\nThe most searched slang of 2025 barely shows up. “6-7,” at 141,000 searches, appears in one comment. “Huzz,” at 61,000, appears in none. Meanwhile a 450-term slang glossary was being sold on X with the pitch “You don’t know the language? You’re invisible.” (1,378 likes).\nThe words they do use are verdicts on the work. They apply them to a scene, a fight or an actor: “Deadass that was goated acting,” 41,254 likes. Coming from a brand, the same words read as decoration. The report also cites a 2025 Journal of Marketing Research study in which slang in brands’ posts cost them likes and sales, in every age group.\n\n## The voice rules that follow\n\n- Play along. Reply to their bit. Don’t start one. The top comment under a bit gets a median of 242 likes; under a sincere post, 3.\n- Tell, don’t ask. A statement with a time in it. Questions are 3.9% of what the room rewards.\n- Hand over the mic. Let a fan’s line be the caption.\n- No glossary. No slang in the caption, and no hashtag stack. Write like the brand’s best account already does.\n\nThe 42x view gap behind the first rule is covered in this guide.\nOne model line from their own rooms shows the second rule: “It should not even be a question. We tee off at X o’clock.” A statement, a time, and the assumption that you’re coming. Under one trailer, “same time next year?” took 4,938 likes: the fans set the cadence themselves. The brand’s job is to say when, and then show up.\n\n## A caption check you can use tomorrow\n\n- Five to eight words. Cut the rest.\n- No hashtag, unless the platform needs one to find the post.\n- No word from a slang list. If you looked it up, cut it.\n- No question to the audience. Say when, and what.\n- Could a fan have written it? If not, find a fan’s line and use that.\n- Read it aloud to someone in the audience. If they wince, start over.\n\nQuestions to ask your social agency: How long are our captions compared with our audience’s comments? How many carry a hashtag? Which of our posts got a reply like “who wrote this”? Who on the team writes our best-performing lines?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Trusting a glossary. The most searched words were nearly absent from the comments.\n- Reading sarcasm as negative. “This looks bad. I’ll be watching.” means “I’m in.”\n- Measuring the audience and not the brand. The gap only shows when both are counted the same way.\n- Asking the room a question. Questions were 3.9% of the top comments. A caption that asks “who’s watching tonight?” asks for something the audience rarely gives.\n\nThe counts describe one audience, men 18 to 24, on public posts in September 2026. One reader labeled the 480 hand-sorted comments, and every label is filed. Instagram returns no like counts on comments, so Instagram examples carry none. The counts show how the audience writes and what it rewards. They don’t prove a caption change will move viewing. Lining your posts up with your own numbers comes next. To start on your own audience, bring us one question.\n\n## Facebook watches but doesn’t share. What to post where\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/facebook-watches-but-doesnt-share\nShort answer: In one brand’s data, Facebook audiences watched useful, repeatable tricks and almost never passed anything on. In a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, Facebook posts averaged 1.2 shares each, 24 of 29 posts got zero or one, and the biggest post on either platform, a how-to with 37,836 views, got none. Post watchable tricks on both platforms, and give Instagram the friend-group jokes made to be sent.\n\n## The two platforms side by side\n\nA hard seltzer brand ran one summer campaign on Instagram and Facebook. Arcus watched every post, sorted each one by what was on screen, and read views and shares from the brand’s own social export. Platforms were never added together. See the content report.\nInstagramFacebook\nPosts3929\nAverage views per post8,1483,230\nAverage shares per post361.2\nBiggest post by viewsSlushie how-to, 32,910Slushie how-to, 37,836\nMost shared postA joke about friends, 409 sharesCans in hot dog buns, 12 shares\n\nSame brand, same summer, often the same video. On Facebook, 24 of 29 posts got zero or one share.\nWhy sort by what’s on screen? Because the campaign name tells you what the brief intended, and the screen tells you what the viewer got. Sorted by the screen, the same pattern appeared on both platforms for the tricks, and two very different patterns appeared for everything else.\n\n## What Facebook watched\n\nThe how-to won. The slushie post, which turned the can into a frozen drink you could make at home, did 37,836 views on Facebook. That’s 11.7 times the Facebook average and the biggest post on either platform. It got zero shares.\nHosting hacks, the group of posts built on a trick with the can, led on Facebook views with a median of 4,509, 1.4 times the average. It led on Instagram too. That’s the one pattern that worked on both platforms. On Facebook, though, the slushie post carried 80% of the group’s views. The other three hosting posts did 6,832, 2,186 and 686 views.\nOne still image got passed on. Cans tucked into hot dog buns did 11,388 views and 12 shares, the only Facebook post in double digits. It never ran on Instagram.\nPut those together and Facebook’s taste is clear in this data. A how-to with a step you can repeat. A product doing something silly in a single frame. Things that are useful or quick to get. The posts that depended on a shared moment, or on knowing who your friends are, didn’t travel there.\n\n## What didn’t travel to Facebook\n\nInstagram’s biggest share hits didn’t carry over. A joke about the viewer’s friends going back to work after a big summer tournament took 409 shares on Instagram, 11 times the Instagram average for shares. On Facebook it did 415 views and one share. The three tournament posts did 739, 598 and 415 views there. The most viewed of them on Instagram, a nine-slide carousel with 29,864 views, never ran on Facebook at all.\nThe report’s read: sharing about a moment people were in, with friends in the joke, is an Instagram behavior for this brand. On Facebook, the same audience watches what’s useful and keeps it to itself.\n\n## What to post where\n\nWhat’s on screenOn InstagramOn FacebookDo this\nA trick you could copy tonight2.36x average views1.4x average viewsPost on both. Lead Facebook with it.\nA joke about friends or a shared momentTop shares on the accountUnder 1,000 viewsInstagram first.\nThe can as the characterShares when the can does something on screenThe only double-digit share postBoth. Try stills on Facebook.\nUnusual places with nothing happening0.30x average views0.13x average viewsRethink. Make something happen.\n3D animationLowest on the accountNot in the Facebook setStop.\n\nThe point isn’t that Facebook is weak. It delivered the single biggest post of the summer. It does a different job: reach for useful things. Planning it like a smaller Instagram wastes that.\nA simple way to test it: take one friend-group post that worked on Instagram and run it on both platforms in the same week, alongside a new how-to made for Facebook. Compare each platform against its own average. One round won’t settle it, but two or three will show you whether the pattern holds for your brand.\n\n## A checklist for your next plan\n\n- Pull views and shares per post for each platform separately. Work out each platform’s own average.\n- Sort posts by what’s on screen, and give each group a median on each platform.\n- Mark the groups that lead on views on both platforms. Those are your safe cross-posts.\n- Mark the posts that took shares on one platform and died on the other. Plan those for the platform that shares.\n- On a platform where most posts get zero or one share, judge on views. Ignore share rate there.\n- Try one format made for the platform that watches: a still, a how-to, a step people can repeat.\n\nQuestions to ask your social agency: Do you report each platform against its own average? Which of our posts ran on one platform only, and why? Which format would you make only for Facebook?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Adding platforms together. A combined number hides that one platform shares and the other doesn’t.\n- Judging Facebook on Instagram’s winners. The posts that won Instagram on shares were among the weakest on Facebook. Read each platform on its own terms.\n- Ranking Facebook by share rate. With 24 of 29 posts at zero or one share, the rate ranks groups on single shares. It’s noise.\n- Cross-posting everything. Sixteen Facebook posts used the same video as an Instagram post. Some of the best Instagram posts never made it over, and some did nothing there.\n\nThe samples are small. Every Facebook group is eight posts or fewer, and groups under four posts are directional. Three Facebook photo posts couldn’t be fetched and took their Instagram twin’s label on an exact caption match. Share counts come from the brand’s social tool; Instagram exposes no share count to any tool, so they can’t be checked against the platform. This is one brand, one summer. It shows what happened on each platform. It doesn’t show why, and it doesn’t show which posts sold. For that, posts need lining up with your own numbers. Our guide to watched versus shared covers the share math. To see what your own platforms do, bring us one question.\n\n## Watched or shared? The hosting hack pattern and the share-rate math\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/watched-vs-shared-share-rate\nShort answer: Views tell you a post got watched; shares tell you someone sent it on, and the two reward different things. In a real Arcus content report, hosting hacks led Instagram views at 2.4 times the average but sat below average on shares, while a joke about the viewer’s friends took 409 shares on 8,000 views. Read shares per 1,000 views next to views, because a high rate on a small post can mean very little.\n\n## Two jobs, two numbers\n\nA view means someone stopped. A share means someone thought of a friend and sent it. Both are good. They’re different jobs, and in a real Arcus content report for a hard seltzer brand, the posts that did one were rarely the posts that did the other. See the content report.\nOn Instagram the account averaged 8,148 views and 36 shares per post across 39 posts. Here are the top four posts by each measure.\nTop four by viewsViewsSharesTop four by sharesViewsShares\nSlushie how-to32,91049Friends back at work after the tournament8,000409\nTournament carousel29,86446A sign held up from a balcony16,713201\nExpiration-date photo25,65734The can as a character22,834138\nThe can as a character22,834138Foil fruit11,64069\n\nOnly one post is on both lists.\nThat post is the exception that explains the rule. The can was the character, on screen the whole time, with the joke happening to it. It was watched like a trick and sent like a joke. The report found the same thing in the next two most shared product posts: the can doing something on screen, not sitting in a hand.\n\n## The hosting hack pattern\n\nThe group that got watched was hosting hacks: a trick with the can you could copy tonight. On Instagram its median was 19,188 views, 2.36 times the account average. It led on Facebook too, at 1.4 times. The three biggest hosting posts showed the can frozen, disguised or turned into dessert. Each showed a step you could repeat at home.\nNow look at its shares. The median was 26, below the account’s 36. People watched the trick and mostly didn’t send it. That’s fine. It’s the reach engine. The report’s call was to keep it and make more: a physical trick with the can that someone could copy tonight.\nShares came from somewhere else:\n\n- A joke about the viewer’s friends. A guy in a team shirt mopes from couch to stoop, can in hand, with one line of text about his friends going back to work. 409 shares.\n- A moment people were in. The sign on the balcony: one line of text, and the joke reads with the sound off. 201 shares.\n- The can as the character. Product posts shared when the can was doing something on screen: 138, 69 and 43 shares for the top three.\n\n## The share-rate math\n\nShare rate puts shares on the same scale as views. The easiest version to read is shares per 1,000 views:\nshares ÷ views × 1,000 = shares per 1,000 views\nWorked from the report’s numbers:\nPostViewsSharesShares per 1,000 views\nFriends back at work8,00040951\nBalcony sign16,71320112\nThe can as a character22,8341386\nSlushie how-to32,910491.5\n\nThe friends post was sent on at 51 per 1,000 views. Nothing else on the account was above about 12. That’s a different kind of post, and the rate shows it.\nWhy per 1,000 views and not shares alone? Because views and shares grow together. A post with four times the views can collect more shares without being any more shareable. Dividing by views takes reach out of the comparison and leaves the question you care about: of the people who saw it, how many sent it on?\n\n## When share rate misleads\n\nA rate with a small number underneath it jumps around. Here are the median share rates by group on Instagram, next to median views:\nGroupMedian viewsMedian share rate\n3D animation (2 posts)1,1390.9%\nWorkday escape3,9620.52%\nUnusual hangouts2,4150.49%\nCan tricks and gags6,1070.21%\nHosting hacks19,1880.14%\n\nRanked by share rate alone, 3D animation wins and hosting hacks comes last. Ranked by views, it’s nearly the reverse. The 3D group was two posts at the bottom of the account, and the report retired it. A few shares on a small post make a big rate. Facebook is the extreme case: with 24 of 29 posts at zero or one share, the report called share rate noise there. More on that in what to post where.\n\n## How to read both: a checklist\n\nHigh share rateLow share rate\nHigh viewsRare. Make more, fast.Watched. Keep it for reach. Test a friend-group frame on top.\nLow viewsCheck the count underneath. Run more before you call it.Stop or rethink.\n\n- Work out views and shares per 1,000 views for every post, per platform.\n- Put both on the group scorecard, with the median for each.\n- Ignore share rate where most posts have zero or one share.\n- Check how many posts sit behind each group. Under four is directional.\n- For your best reach format, write one version with the viewer’s friends in the joke and compare its share rate.\n\nThat last step is the report’s own test. Run hosting hacks and the can posts with a friend-group frame on top, the kind of setup where the next move is to send it to someone, and watch whether the share rate moves while views hold.\nQuestions to ask your social agency: Do you report shares per 1,000 views, by platform? Which posts got watched and which got sent? What would you change about our best-watched format to make it shareable?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Chasing the one viral share post. Name what it had, a moment or a friend, and test that on formats you already make well.\n- Ranking groups on share rate alone. Small posts win on paper.\n- Treating shares as sales. A share is a strong signal that someone cared. It’s not a purchase.\n- Forgetting the platform. The friends post took 409 shares on Instagram and one on Facebook. Rates belong to one platform at a time.\n\nShare counts here come from the brand’s social tool. Instagram exposes no share count to any tool, so they can’t be checked against the platform. The tournament group was three posts and directional, and the tournament is over: the real test is whether the next big shared moment does the same. None of this shows which posts sold. That takes lining posts up with your own numbers. To see that on your own posts, bring us one question, like “Which of our shared posts moved sales?”\n\n## Does retail eat online sales? What five brands that moved into retail show\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/does-retail-eat-online-sales\nShort answer: Not at any of the five brands in a real Arcus case series: Hero, Bubble, Harry’s, Liquid Death and Poppi. The online share of sales shifted as retail grew, but demand didn’t move away; industry research across $848 billion of transactions found a new retail location lifts nearby online sales 6.9%, and 13.9% for direct-to-consumer brands. What retail does eat is margin: the retailer’s cut, trade spend, slotting and deductions.\n\n## The answer, brand by brand\n\nA skincare brand moving into retail asked Arcus a question before writing its media plan: will the shelf eat our online sales? Arcus studied five direct-to-consumer brands that made the same move, from public sources, with every number traced to a page. The answer came as ten slides, a list of all 157 sources, and 14 more slides showing the source page behind each number, captured on September 10, 2026. See the market report.\nBrandWhat happened to the online business as retail scaled\nHeroAmazon was still the majority of revenue at the $630 million sale. First in its Amazon category in 2024.\nBubbleThe site became the community and launch channel. Over $100 million a year across 17,000-plus doors (retail locations).\nHarry’sAlmost 500,000 subscribers added at 80% repeat in the Target launch year. Target sold the subscription.\nLiquid DeathDropped its own online fulfillment by choice and sent site shoppers to Amazon and retail. $333 million of retail sales in 2024.\nPoppiStill the top-selling soft drink on Amazon past 30,000 doors. 2024 growth came from volume where it was already sold.\n\nRetail didn’t cannibalize online demand at any of the five. The share shifted, and the online business grew or, in Liquid Death’s case, was handed to Amazon and retail on purpose. The cannibalization that is real is margin: the retailer’s cut, trade spend (what a brand pays retailers for promotions and placement), slotting fees (what it pays to get on the shelf) and deductions.\n\n## Why the two channels fed each other\n\n- The halo is measurable. A new retail location lifts online sales in its area by 6.9%, and by 13.9% for direct-to-consumer brands, according to industry research across $848 billion of transactions.\n- One price, different counts by channel. Hero holds $12.99 everywhere and changes the count. Bubble prices Walmart three cents under its own site.\n- The site keeps a job the shelf can’t do. Harry’s subscription, Hero’s community reviews, Bubble’s ambassadors, Poppi’s founder posts.\n- Amazon is the signal buyers read. Retailers “see Amazon success as validation.” Poppi’s Amazon rank went into its line reviews, the meetings where a retailer decides which products stay on the shelf.\n- The shelf reaches people the site never did. Two thirds of Harry’s early Target buyers had never bought a razor at Target.\n\n## What the media did\n\nThe launches that worked pointed media at one retailer’s shelf, in the week the product landed there.\n\n- Hero at Target, 2019. TikTok videos named the retailer and asked people to photograph the shelf. It “helped to double Hero’s sales goals at Target,” at seven times the cost-efficiency of Instagram Stories. Target then expanded the range.\n- Bubble at Walmart and Target. Ambassadors’ faces on the Walmart end-of-aisle displays, a Snapchat filter and live TikTok in launch week. At Target, a 380,598-person list at a 74% open rate, sent to one shelf display in one week.\n- Poppi, from Amazon to 30,000 doors. One founder video: “over $100,000 on Amazon while we were sleeping.” The Amazon rank became the slide in the line review. TikTok carried most of the paid media.\n\nRetail media was bought for position. Hero’s attributed sales on Target’s network rose 824% ten months after joining, in a vendor’s case study. The report also cites 247% in attributed sales across newer retail media marketplaces, and one line sums up the approach: “We’re willing to accept a lower ROAS on these new marketplaces.” What the networks sell is rank in the retailer’s own search and measurement against the retailer’s own transactions. Those attributed sales land in the buyer’s dashboard. The member bases are large: more than 100 million Target Circle members, about 44 million Ulta members, and more than 90 million CVS ExtraCare members.\n\n## The four numbers the buyer reads\n\nRetail buyers judge a brand on four numbers: ACV (how much of the retailer’s volume sits in doors that carry you), units per door per week against the category, household penetration, and repeat. A count of doors is a count. These are what buyers manage to.\nHarry’s published its early read: 10% of Target’s cartridge sales and about 50% of handles within four weeks, then sales and penetration tripled by month eight. Those numbers helped sell Walmart. Poppi’s growth at 30,000 doors came from volume where it was already sold, with repeat over 40%. That growth is what PepsiCo paid $1.95 billion for.\nThe point for a media plan: the buyer reads these numbers at the next review, and the next retailer reads them before saying yes. Media that moves one retailer’s sales rate in the week the product lands shows up in all four. Media that only moves impressions shows up in none of them.\n\n## Five moves for the media plan, and what to ask\n\n- Point launch media at one retailer’s shelf in the week your product lands there. Measure the lift in that retailer’s sales rate, not impressions.\n- Fund retail media from the trade budget, network by network. Buy search rank and new-to-brand share. Accept a lower ROAS for position.\n- Give the site and Amazon jobs the shelf can’t do. A subscription or a drop, reviews syndicated to every listing, one price with counts by channel.\n- Report ACV, sales rate, penetration and repeat. Publish a four-week read after the next launch, the way Harry’s did.\n- Audit product claims before the media runs. The comment section follows the product onto every retailer’s review tab. Poppi’s claim cost $8.9 million.\n\nQuestions to ask your agency before a retail launch: Which retailer and which week is the launch media pointed at? How will you show that retailer’s sales rate moved? Is retail media coming from the trade budget or the brand budget? Which of our claims would hold up on a review tab?\n\n## Common mistakes, and what this doesn’t prove\n\n- Planning for lost demand that the cases don’t show. Plan for margin instead.\n- Reporting doors. Buyers manage to the four numbers.\n- Different prices on different channels. The cases held one price and changed the count.\n- Judging retail media on ROAS alone. The cases bought it for rank and accepted a lower return.\n\nThese are five brands that succeeded, studied from public filings, interviews, trade press, retailer media networks and marketplace listings. Brands that moved into retail and struggled are less likely to publish. The 824% figure is a vendor’s case study. The halo figures are averages across many brands, not a forecast for yours. The cases show what happened. Whether your launch media moves a retailer’s sales rate is something your own numbers can show, launch by launch, once retailer data, sales and media sit in one layer. To start, bring us one question, like “Did our launch media move the retailer’s sales rate?”\n\n## How to check your Meta agency (Facebook and Instagram)\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-meta-agency\nShort answer: Start by asking for Meta’s results split into clicks and views, with the window behind each. Then add Meta’s claims to every other agency’s and compare the total with your own numbers. For proof that Meta caused results, run ads in some regions and not others for a few weeks.\n\n## How a Meta recap counts results\n\nYour Meta agency reports from Meta Ads Manager. The results in that report are the ones Meta credits to its own ads, using Meta’s own rule for attribution. Meta can see what happened on Facebook and Instagram and, through its tracking on your site or app, some of what happened next. It can’t see your TikTok ads, your emails, or the creator video someone watched on YouTube.\nTwo settings shape every number in the recap. The first is the conversion window: how many days after someone clicked or saw an ad a result still counts. The second is whether views count at all. A view-through conversion is a result credited to an ad someone saw and never clicked. Meta reports often combine clicks and views into one number. Check the window in the report before you read anything else.\nMeta also estimates some results it can’t observe directly, because phones and browsers share less than they used to. That’s a reasonable fix for missing data. It also means part of the number is a model, and the recap rarely says which part.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on Meta\n\nMeta is usually the biggest claim in a recap, so it’s where double counting shows up first. Here’s where it comes from:\n\n- View-throughs. Facebook and Instagram reach a lot of people. Many of them buy for other reasons in the days after seeing an ad, and a view rule credits Meta with those results.\n- Partnership ads. When your media agency puts spend behind a creator’s post, the results can show up in the media agency’s report and the creator agency’s report. See creator whitelisting.\n- Retargeting. Ads shown to people who already visited your site catch buyers close to a decision. Those results often belong to whatever brought them the first time.\n- Your own channels. A buyer who opens your email and buys can still be claimed by Meta if they saw an ad that week.\n\nNone of this means the agency is doing bad work. It means Meta’s number is Meta’s view, and needs to sit next to everyone else’s.\n\n## A checklist for your next Meta recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nWindowThe click and view windows behind the resultsLonger windows reach further back for credit\nClicks vs. viewsResults split by click and by viewView-throughs are the easiest results to overcount\nModeled resultsWhether some results are estimatedEstimates should be labeled as estimates\nPartnership adsCreator posts with paid spend behind themTwo agencies may claim the same result\nTotal vs. your numbersMeta’s claims plus every other agency’s, against your own totalA total above what you had proves overlap\nReachHow many different people saw the adsSee reach vs. impressions\n\n## Questions to ask your Meta agency\n\n- What are the click and view windows on this report, and can we see results at click only?\n- How much of this number was estimated by Meta instead of observed?\n- Which results came from partnership ads, and does the creator agency report them too?\n- How much of the spend went to people who had already visited our site?\n- If we added your claims to every other agency’s, would you expect the total to fit inside our own numbers?\n\nGood agencies answer these quickly. Our list of questions to ask before you renew goes further.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on Meta\n\nMatching Meta’s claims against your own numbers removes double counting. It doesn’t prove Meta caused anything. For that, run a holdout test: pick regions that look alike, run Meta ads in some of them, leave the others dark for a few weeks, and compare results using your own numbers.\nMeta’s targeting by location makes this easier than on most channels. The hard part is keeping everything else steady: no extra promotions in one group, no different creative, no surprise press. Agree the length and what you’ll do with each outcome before you start.\nMeta also offers its own lift studies. They’re useful, and worth running. They’re also run by the company selling the ads, so it helps to read them next to a test measured on your own numbers. Our guide to incrementality walks through the steps.\nWant to see how your Meta recap holds up first? Bring it to us as your first question, free. We check it against your own numbers and walk you through it on a 30-minute call.\n\n## How to check your TikTok agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-tiktok-agency\nShort answer: Ask for TikTok’s results split into clicks and views, with the window behind each, and a list of any boosted creator posts. Add TikTok’s claims to every other agency’s and compare the total with your own numbers. To prove TikTok caused results, hold out some regions for a few weeks.\n\n## How a TikTok recap counts results\n\nA TikTok recap shows the results TikTok credits to its own ads. Like every platform, TikTok uses its own attribution rule and only sees what its own tracking can see. It doesn’t know about your email, your Meta ads or the Reddit thread someone read the night before.\nThe numbers on top are usually views, watch time and engagement. Those tell you how the ads landed with the people who saw them. Results come lower down, often as one number that blends people who clicked with people who only watched. A view-through conversion counts a result for someone who saw the ad and never tapped. Check the window in the report: how many days after a view or click a result still counts.\nTikTok recaps also love engagement rate. It’s a useful number for creative, and it isn’t a result. Ask what the rate is divided by, because views, reach and followers give very different answers.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on TikTok\n\n- Boosted creator posts. When an agency puts spend behind a creator’s video, the creator agency and the media agency can both claim its results. This is the most common overlap on TikTok.\n- Creator codes. A creator code from a TikTok video can spread to coupon sites, where people who were already buying find it.\n- First touch, last claim. TikTok is often where someone first hears of you. They may buy a week later through search or email, and TikTok’s view rule can still claim the result.\n- Views at scale. TikTok serves a lot of views. Even a small share of viewers who buy for other reasons adds up to a big view-through number.\n\nThe fix starts with one list. Take your own numbers for the month, then mark each result with every agency that claimed it. Results with two or more names are the overlap. TikTok usually shares a lot of them with creators and Meta, because the same people see all three in the same week. Once you can see the overlap, the conversation with each agency gets shorter and friendlier.\n\n## A checklist for your next TikTok recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nWindowClick and view windows behind each resultView rules reach far on a platform this big\nClicks vs. viewsResults split by tap and by viewShows how much rests on views alone\nBoosted postsCreator videos with paid spendTwo agencies may claim them\nCodesCode uses by sourceCoupon sites inflate code results\nEngagement rateWhat it’s divided byViews, reach and followers give different rates\nCommentsWhat people said under the adsTells you how the audience took it\n\n## Questions to ask your TikTok agency\n\n- Can we see results split by click and by view, with the window for each?\n- Which results came from boosted creator posts, and does the creator agency report them too?\n- How many code uses came from coupon sites?\n- Is engagement rate divided by views, reach or followers?\n- What did people say in the comments, and did it change the creative?\n\nThe comments are worth reading on their own. TikTok audiences say what they think, and they say it fast. Our guide to listening that counts shows how to turn comments into numbers you can use.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on TikTok\n\nMatching removes double counting. It can’t tell you whether TikTok caused results. For that, run a holdout test: choose regions that look alike, run TikTok ads in some and not others for a few weeks, and compare results using your own numbers.\nPaid ads can be held out by region. Organic creator posts are harder, because they travel wherever the algorithm sends them. If creators are a big part of your TikTok spend, test the paid boost on their posts by region, and judge the creators themselves on codes, links and timing against your own numbers.\nDecide before you start what you’ll do with each outcome. A test that ends in “interesting” is a test nobody acts on.\nIf you want to see what your TikTok recap looks like checked against your own numbers, bring it to us as your first question, free. Our list of questions to ask before you renew covers creators too.\n\n## How to check your Google Search agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-google-search-agency\nShort answer: Ask your search agency to split results into searches for your brand’s name and everything else. Brand search mostly catches people already on their way to you. To find out what it adds, pause brand search in some regions for a few weeks and compare results with your own numbers.\n\n## How a search recap counts results\n\nYour search agency reports from Google Ads. The results it shows are the ones Google credits to its ads, using whichever attribution model the account is set to. Search is the last step for a lot of buyers: they hear about you somewhere else, type your name, click the top result and buy. That puts search in a perfect spot to collect credit.\nThe most important split in any search report is brand versus non-brand. Brand search means ads shown to people who typed your name. Non-brand means ads shown for things like “hard seltzer near me” or “new horror movies.” Brand searches tend to show excellent results. Most of those people were already looking for you.\nSearch accounts also have settings that change the count: the conversion window, the attribution model, and which actions count as a result. Some accounts import results from Google Analytics as well as tracking them directly. Check the window in the report, and ask where each result comes from.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on search\n\n- Brand search. Someone saw a TikTok, a trailer or a billboard, then searched your name. Search claims the result. So does the channel that sent them.\n- Mixed campaign types. Some Google campaign types run across search, YouTube, display and more at once. They can include searches for your name inside the mix. Ask for a split.\n- Two sources for one result. If an account counts a result from its own tag and an imported one from Google Analytics, the same result can show up twice in one report.\n- Email and direct. A buyer who clicks your email, leaves, then comes back through a search ad gets credited to search.\n\nLast-click rules make all of this bigger, because they hand everything to the final step. Even accounts that use other models tend to reward search well.\n\nA quick way to see the size of it: ask for one month of results split by brand and non-brand, then set the brand number next to what your other agencies claimed that month. If social, creators and search all claim a big share, and the total runs past your own numbers, brand search is likely where much of the overlap sits.\n\n## A checklist for your next search recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nBrand vs. non-brandResults split by searches for your nameBrand search catches people already on their way\nMixed campaignsWhether brand searches sit inside broad campaign typesBrand credit can hide in the mix\nResult sourcesWhich actions count, and where they’re trackedTwo sources can count one result twice\nModel and windowAttribution model and conversion windowBoth change how much credit search gets\nTotal vs. your numbersSearch claims plus everyone else’s, against your totalOverlap shows up in the sum\n\n## Questions to ask your search agency\n\n- How much of what you claimed came from people searching our name?\n- Do any campaigns include brand searches without labeling them?\n- Which attribution model and window does the account use?\n- Is any result counted from both the Google Ads tag and Google Analytics?\n- What do you think would happen if we paused brand search in one region?\n\nThat last question is a good one to ask out loud. An agency that welcomes a test is usually confident in the work.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on search\n\nBrand search is the cleanest place to start testing, because the question is simple: if we stopped paying for clicks on our own name, would those people still find us? Run a holdout test. Pause brand search in some regions, keep it on in matched regions, wait a few weeks, and compare results using your own numbers.\nTwo things to watch. Competitors may bid on your name while you’re paused, so note whether that happens. And keep everything else steady: no new promotions in one group only.\nIf results barely move in the paused regions, brand search was mostly collecting credit. If they drop, it was doing real work, and you have proof. Either way you’ve learned something worth more than the clicks. Our guide to incrementality without the math has a worked example.\nWant to see how much of your search agency’s credit is brand search? Bring the recap to us as your first question, free.\n\n## How to check your YouTube agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-youtube-agency\nShort answer: Ask your YouTube agency which results came from people who clicked and which from people who only watched, and over what window. Ask whether the view counts include paid views. Then add YouTube’s claims to every other agency’s and compare with your own numbers.\n\n## How a YouTube recap counts results\n\nYouTube ads are bought through Google Ads, so a YouTube recap uses Google’s attribution rules. The top of the report is usually views, view rate and watch time. Those tell you how the ad played. The results come further down, and they can include people who watched part of an ad without clicking. Google calls some of these engaged-view conversions. They work like a view-through conversion: credit for watching, with no click.\nThe other thing to know is that a view on YouTube can mean different things. A paid view, a view on your channel and a view on a creator’s channel aren’t the same. Ask whether the view count on a video includes paid views before you compare it with your organic posts.\nCheck the window in the report. Video is often seen days before someone acts, so the window decides how much later activity YouTube can claim.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on YouTube\n\n- Watching, then searching. Someone watches a trailer on YouTube, then searches your title and clicks a search ad. YouTube and search can both claim the result, sometimes inside the same Google account.\n- Creator videos. A creator agency claims the integration in a creator’s video. If the media agency also ran ads on that video or that channel, both may claim it.\n- Long windows on big reach. Video reaches many people, and a long window lets YouTube claim anyone who later acts for any reason.\n- Mixed campaign types. Some Google campaign types spread across YouTube, search and more. Ask for YouTube on its own.\n\nA simple first step: ask for one month of YouTube results split into clicks and watches, then line them up against your own numbers by day. If results rise on the days a trailer or ad drops, that’s a good sign. If the claimed number stays high on quiet days, the window is probably doing a lot of the work.\n\n## A checklist for your next YouTube recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nClicks vs. watchesResults split by click and by watched-without-clickWatch-based credit is easy to overcount\nWindowHow many days after a view a result still countsVideo is often seen long before action\nPaid vs. organic viewsWhether view counts include paid viewsPaid views can make organic posts look stronger\nWatch timeHow long people kept watchingTells you whether the ad held attention\nOverlap with searchResults claimed by both YouTube and searchSame account, two claims\nCommentsWhat viewers said under the videoThe fastest read on how it landed\n\nBring these questions to the next review rather than over email. Answers in the room tend to be clearer, and you’ll hear which ones the agency can answer straight away.\n\n## Questions to ask your YouTube agency\n\n- How many of these results came from people who clicked, and how many from people who only watched?\n- What window are you using, and can we see a shorter one?\n- Does the view count on our trailer include paid views?\n- Are any of these results also claimed by our search campaigns?\n- Which videos kept people watching longest, and did those drive more results?\n\nComments are worth a look too. In a real Arcus audience report for a streaming service, fans talked about the shows and almost never about the service itself. Our guide on watched vs. shared explains why views alone can mislead.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on YouTube\n\nYouTube ads can be targeted by location, so a holdout test works the same way as on other channels. Pick regions that look alike, run YouTube in some and not others for a few weeks, and compare your own numbers: tickets, sign-ups, streams or sales.\nKeep search steady during the test, since the two interact. If you pause YouTube and search results fall in the same regions, that tells you something useful about how they work together.\nGoogle also offers lift studies for YouTube, including surveys that measure recall. A brand lift study is good evidence for brand work. It measures what people say in a survey, so it’s strongest when it lines up with your own numbers later.\nIf you’d like a second look at a YouTube recap, bring it to us as your first question, free. We check it against your own numbers and walk you through it.\n\n## How to check your Snapchat agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-snapchat-agency\nShort answer: Ask your agency for Snapchat’s results split into swipes or clicks and views, with the window behind each. Snapchat often reaches the same people as TikTok and Instagram, so add every platform’s claims together and compare the total with your own numbers. Hold out some regions to prove cause.\n\n## How a Snapchat recap counts results\n\nA Snapchat recap shows what Snapchat credits to its own ads, using its own attribution rule. Results can be credited to people who swiped or tapped on an ad, and to people who only saw it. A view-through conversion is the second kind. The recap often blends them, so ask for a split.\nSnapchat reports also lean on reach, impressions and swipe rate. Those tell you how many people saw the ad and how many acted on it right away. They aren’t results. Before you compare them with other channels, check reach vs. impressions: impressions count repeats, reach counts people.\nAnd check the window in the report. It decides how many days after a view or swipe a result still counts.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on Snapchat\n\n- The same young audience everywhere. Snapchat, TikTok and Instagram often reach the same people in the same week. Each platform can claim the same buyer.\n- Views on fast content. Snaps are quick and seen often. A view rule can credit Snapchat with results that happened for other reasons.\n- Codes and links. If a creator promotes you on Snapchat with a code, and the code spreads, the creator agency and the media agency may both claim the use.\n- Lenses and filters. Branded lenses get lots of plays. Plays are a reach number. Ask how any results tied to them were counted.\n\nThe way to see it is to stop reading each platform’s report on its own. Put Snapchat’s claims, TikTok’s claims and Meta’s claims for the same month side by side, add them up and compare the total with your own numbers. If the three together claim more than you had, the overlap is real. Then the question becomes which platform reached people the others missed, and that’s a question a test can answer.\n\nIt also helps to look at timing. If Snapchat’s claimed results rise and fall with your TikTok flights, the two are probably reaching the same people.\n\n## A checklist for your next Snapchat recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nSwipes vs. viewsResults split by swipe and by viewShows how much rests on views\nWindowDays after a view or swipe a result countsLonger windows reach further back\nReachDifferent people reachedImpressions count repeats\nOverlapSnapchat’s claims plus TikTok and Meta claimsSame audience, several claims\nLensesHow results from lenses were countedPlays aren’t results\n\nNone of these questions is a trap. They’re the same ones you’d ask any platform, and an agency that knows its account can answer them in a day. If an answer takes weeks, or never comes, that tells you something about how closely the account is being read.\n\n## Questions to ask your Snapchat agency\n\n- How many results came from swipes, and how many from views alone?\n- What window does this report use?\n- How many different people did the campaign reach, and how often did each see it?\n- Were any of these results also claimed in our TikTok or Meta reports?\n- How were results from lenses or filters counted, if at all?\n\nIf Snapchat is a smaller line in your budget, these questions still matter. A small budget can claim a large share of results, and that’s exactly when a check helps.\n\nKeep the agency in the loop on all of this. Most media buyers want to know which platforms are earning their place, and a shared count gives them a better brief.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on Snapchat\n\nSnapchat ads can be targeted by location, so the holdout test is the same idea as anywhere else. Pick regions that look alike. Run Snapchat in some and not others for a few weeks. Compare results using your own numbers.\nBecause Snapchat overlaps so much with TikTok and Instagram, keep those steady in both groups during the test. If they shift at the same time, you won’t know which one moved results.\nIf the regions without Snapchat look the same as the ones with it, Snapchat was mostly reaching people other channels already had. If they fall behind, you’ve found what Snapchat adds. Our guide to incrementality walks through how to set it up.\nNot sure where to start? Bring your most recent recap to us as your first question, free. We check it against your own numbers and show you what held up.\n\n## How to check your Reddit agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-reddit-agency\nShort answer: Check a Reddit recap in two parts. First, match Reddit’s claimed results against your own numbers and every other agency’s claims, with clicks and views split. Second, read the comments on your promoted posts and the threads around them. Reddit users talk back, and that tells you how the ads landed.\n\n## How a Reddit recap counts results\n\nA Reddit recap shows the results Reddit credits to its own ads, using its own attribution rule and tracking. Like other platforms, it can credit results to people who clicked and to people who only saw a post. Ask for the split, and check the window in the report.\nReddit recaps also show which communities and interests each campaign targeted, plus upvotes and comments on promoted posts. Upvotes are a reaction. Comments are where the information is. On Reddit, people say what they think of an ad right under it, and other people vote on whether they agree.\nReddit is different from most channels in one useful way: a lot of the conversation about your brand already happens there, unpaid. That gives you something to compare the ads against.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on Reddit\n\n- Research, then purchase. People often read Reddit threads before buying, then buy through search or your site. Reddit’s view rule and search’s click rule can both claim that buyer.\n- Organic threads. A popular thread about your brand can drive results with no ad involved. If an ad ran the same week, Reddit may claim them.\n- Views without clicks. Reddit users scroll a lot. A view-through rule can credit Reddit with results from people who barely noticed the ad.\n- Other agencies’ reports. As with any channel, Reddit’s claims belong in the same count as everyone else’s. See double counting.\n\nReddit claims are often small next to Meta or search, which makes them easy to skip. Don’t. A small channel that claims a lot of results for little spend is worth a look, and a small channel that reaches people nobody else does is worth protecting. The only way to tell the two apart is to count each result once across every agency and see what’s left for Reddit.\n\n## A checklist for your next Reddit recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nClicks vs. viewsResults split by eachShows how much rests on scrolling past\nWindowDays after a view or click a result countsResearch-heavy buyers act later\nCommunitiesWhere the ads ranCompare with where your audience talks unpaid\nCommentsWhat people said under promoted postsThe clearest read on how the ad landed\nOrganic threadsPopular threads about you in the same weeksThey can drive results an ad gets credit for\n\nAsk for the comments as a file, with the post each one sits under. Reading them in one place, instead of scrolling post by post, makes the patterns easier to see, and it gives you something to count.\n\n## Questions to ask your Reddit agency\n\n- How many of these results came from clicks, and how many from views?\n- Which communities did the best, and why did you pick them?\n- What did people say in the comments, and did you change anything because of it?\n- Were there big organic threads about us during the campaign?\n- Would you expect your claims plus every other agency’s to fit inside our own numbers?\n\nThen read the room. Reddit is one of the best places to hear how your audience talks when you’re not in the room. Social listening on Reddit means reading the threads where people discuss your category and counting what comes up: the complaints, the comparisons, the words they use. Those counts tell you whether your ads sound like the people you want to reach. Our guide to listening that counts explains how.\n\nListening also gives your agency better material. If the threads keep coming back to one flavor, one character or one complaint, that’s a brief for the next promoted post.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on Reddit\n\nReddit ads can be targeted by location, so you can run a holdout test: run ads in some regions, leave matched regions alone for a few weeks, and compare your own numbers. Keep your other channels steady while it runs.\nReddit budgets are often smaller, so the test needs regions big enough for results to move. If the difference is too small to read, that’s a finding too: it means Reddit isn’t adding much you can measure yet.\nWant the check and the listening in one answer? Bring us one free question, or see what we listen to on the listening page.\n\n## How to check your Pinterest agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-pinterest-agency\nShort answer: Pinterest is a planning platform, so results often come days or weeks after someone saves a Pin. That makes the window behind each result the first thing to check. Then split clicks from views, add Pinterest’s claims to every other agency’s, and compare the total with your own numbers.\n\n## How a Pinterest recap counts results\n\nA Pinterest recap shows the results Pinterest credits to its ads, using its own attribution rule. People use Pinterest to plan: a party, a wardrobe, a skincare routine, a cocktail menu. They save Pins and come back later. That makes the conversion window unusually important, because it decides how long after a save or click Pinterest can still claim a result. Check the window in the report before anything else.\nPinterest recaps lead with impressions, saves and clicks. Saves are a real sign of interest. They aren’t results. The question is whether saves turn into sales, tickets or sign-ups, and that only shows up in your own numbers.\nAs with other platforms, results can be credited to views as well as clicks. A view-through conversion on a planning platform can reach far back. Ask for the split.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on Pinterest\n\n- Long gaps. The longer the time between a Pin and a result, the more chances another channel had to reach the same person and claim them too.\n- Search at the end. Someone saves your Pin, then searches your name weeks later. Pinterest and search can both claim it.\n- Your own Pins. Organic Pins and paid Pins look alike. Results driven by your own Pins can land in the paid report.\n- Email and retargeting. A planner who signed up for your emails may buy after an email. A long Pinterest window can still claim it.\n\nPut it on one table. List your own results for the month, then mark which ones Pinterest claimed and which ones other agencies claimed. On a planning platform, you’ll often find Pinterest’s results sit late in the window, long after the save, and that other channels touched the same people in between. That doesn’t mean Pinterest did nothing. It started the plan, and starting matters. It means the credit needs sharing, and a test is how you find out how much Pinterest earned.\n\n## A checklist for your next Pinterest recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nWindowDays after a save, click or view a result countsPlanning means long gaps, and long gaps invite overlap\nClicks vs. viewsResults split by eachView credit reaches far on a slow platform\nSaves to resultsWhether saved Pins lead to resultsSaves show interest, and only results show sales\nPaid vs. organicWhich Pins were paidOrganic results can land in paid reports\nTotal vs. your numbersPinterest claims plus everyone else’sOverlap shows up in the sum\n\nIf the agency can show results at a shorter window, compare the two numbers. The gap between them is the share of credit that rests on a long wait, and it’s the share most worth testing before you move budget.\n\n## Questions to ask your Pinterest agency\n\n- What window does this report use for clicks, saves and views?\n- Can we see results at a shorter window?\n- How many results came from views without a click?\n- Do saved Pins lead to more results than Pins people only clicked?\n- Are any results from our own organic Pins included?\n\nThese are good questions for any platform with a slow path to purchase. The guide to reading an agency report in 10 minutes covers the general version.\n\nWhile the test runs, keep an eye on saves in both groups. If saves rise where Pinterest runs and results follow later, you’re seeing the slow path to purchase in action.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on Pinterest\n\nPinterest ads can be targeted by location, so a holdout test works here too. Pick matched regions. Run Pinterest in some and not others. Compare results using your own numbers.\nThe difference on Pinterest is time. Because people plan before they buy, the test needs to run longer than it would on a fast channel, and you should keep measuring for a while after it ends. Agree the length before you start, and write down what you’ll do with each outcome.\nSeasonal brands should time tests with care. A holiday or a summer push changes everything at once, so test in a steadier stretch if you can, and keep other channels the same in both groups.\nWant to know how your Pinterest claims fit with the rest? Bring a recap to us as your first question, free, and we’ll check it against your own numbers.\n\n## How to check your LinkedIn agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-linkedin-agency\nShort answer: Match every lead, registration or sign-up LinkedIn claims against your own records, so each person is counted once. Split results from clicks and from views, and check the window. Then follow the leads forward: what matters is how many became something, which your CRM can show.\n\n## How a LinkedIn recap counts results\n\nEntertainment and lifestyle brands don’t usually sell to consumers on LinkedIn. They use it to reach the trade: buyers, distributors, exhibitors, partners, press and future hires. The results are sign-ups, event registrations and leads, often through LinkedIn’s own lead forms.\nA LinkedIn recap shows the results LinkedIn credits to its ads, using its own attribution rule. Lead forms are filled out inside LinkedIn, so they’re easy for LinkedIn to count. Results on your own site rely on tracking, and can be credited to people who clicked or only saw the ad. Check the window in the report, and ask for clicks and views split.\nLinkedIn audiences are often small and specific. That’s good for targeting. It also means a handful of results can swing the numbers, so look at counts as well as rates.\n\n## Where double counting comes from on LinkedIn\n\n- Lead forms and your CRM. A lead form fill shows up in LinkedIn’s report and, once synced, in your CRM with a source. If someone also registered through your site, they may appear twice.\n- Views at work. Busy people scroll LinkedIn without clicking. A view-through rule can claim them when they register later through an email invite.\n- Email invites. Most event registrations come after an invite. If a LinkedIn ad ran the same week, both can claim the registration.\n- Sales and partnerships teams. Your own team’s outreach brings in leads too. Make sure LinkedIn isn’t claiming those.\n\nThe fix is a single list of people. Pull LinkedIn’s leads, your site registrations and your CRM records for the same campaign, then match them by person. Anyone who appears in two places is counted once. What’s left is a clean count of who LinkedIn brought in, and a clean starting point for asking what those people did next. Our guide to double counting explains why this matters on every channel.\n\n## A checklist for your next LinkedIn recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nLeads vs. CRMLead form fills matched to CRM recordsCounts each person once\nClicks vs. viewsResults split by eachView credit is common on a scroll-heavy feed\nWindowDays after a view or click a result countsTrade decisions take time\nEmail overlapRegistrations that came after an inviteTwo channels, one person\nWhat happened nextLeads that became meetings, deals or attendeesA lead is only the first step\n\nAsk for the lead list in a file you can match yourself. A good agency will send it without fuss. Then ask your own team, the people who follow up on leads, which ones turned into real conversations. Their answer is often the most honest number in the whole review.\n\n## Questions to ask your LinkedIn agency\n\n- How many results were lead forms, and how many were tracked on our site?\n- How many came from views without a click?\n- Can you give us the list of leads so we can match them to our CRM?\n- How many registrants also got an email invite from us?\n- Which of these leads went on to become something?\n\nThe last question is the one that matters most. Our guide to first-party data explains why your own records are the best place to answer it.\n\nShare what you find with the agency. Most LinkedIn buyers would rather optimize for leads that turn into something, and a clean list helps them do it.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like on LinkedIn\n\nLinkedIn ads can be targeted by location and by audience, so a holdout test can split either. For an event, hold out some cities. For a trade campaign, hold out a matched group of companies or job roles and compare sign-ups with your own records.\nSmall audiences make tests harder to read, so pick a question where the result would change a decision: whether to keep a LinkedIn line in next year’s event budget, for example. Run it for a few weeks and keep email invites the same in both groups.\nIf the held-out group registers at the same rate, your invites and your team were doing the work. If it falls behind, LinkedIn earned its place.\nWant a second look at what LinkedIn claims? Bring the recap to us as your first question, free.\n\n## How to check your creator and influencer agency\nURL: https://usearcus.ai/library/check-your-creator-agency\nShort answer: Judge creators on results in your own numbers. Reach and estimated media value come second. Check every code and link for coupon-site leaks, match creator claims against your media agency’s claims for boosted posts, and line up post dates with results. Hold out regions on paid boosts to prove cause.\n\n## How a creator recap counts results\n\nCreator recaps usually lead with reach, views, engagement and an estimated value: what the same attention would have cost as paid media. That estimate is often called earned media value. It’s a way to describe reach in dollars. It isn’t a result, and it rises with views whether or not anyone bought anything.\nResults come from three places. Creator codes and links trace some buyers to a creator. Paid boosts of creator posts, sometimes called whitelisting or partnership ads, get results from the ad platform’s own attribution. And timing: sales, tickets or sign-ups that rose when a creator posted.\nEach of those can be useful. Each can also be stretched. Check how every number in the recap was counted before you compare creators.\n\n## Where double counting comes from with creators\n\n- Codes that leak. Creator codes end up on coupon sites. People who were already buying find them at checkout, and the creator gets credit.\n- Boosted posts. When a media agency puts spend behind a creator’s post, both agencies can claim the results.\n- Timing on a busy week. Launch weeks stack creators, ads, press and email. Every one of them will point to the same spike.\n- Fans who were coming anyway. A creator who already loves your brand often reaches people who already love it too. Their code gets used by regulars.\n\nThe way through is one list. Start from your own numbers for the campaign weeks. Mark which results used a creator code, which came through a boosted post, and which arrived on days a creator posted. Then mark every agency that claimed each one. Results with two names are the overlap. Results with a code from a coupon site go in their own pile. What’s left is what your creators can fairly claim, and it’s usually a better basis for renewals than reach.\n\nDo this creator by creator. Some will look much better than their reach suggested, and some much worse.\n\n## A checklist for your next creator recap\n\nCheckWhat to look forWhy it matters\nCode sourcesWhere code uses came fromCoupon sites inflate creator results\nNew vs. existing buyersCode users who were already customersRegulars would have bought anyway\nBoosted postsPosts with paid spend, and which agency claims themTwo agencies, one result\nEstimated valueHow it was calculatedIt measures reach in dollars\nSharesShares compared with viewsSee share rate\nCommentsWhat the creator’s audience saidShows whether the fit was right\n\nAsk for answers in writing, creator by creator. A table with each creator’s posts, codes, code sources, paid spend and claimed results makes the comparison fair. It also shows which creators the agency knows well and which ones it only booked. If the agency can’t split results that way, that’s worth knowing before the next round of bookings.\n\n## Questions to ask your creator agency\n\n- How many code uses came from coupon sites?\n- How many code users were already our customers?\n- Which posts had paid spend behind them, and does our media agency claim those results too?\n- How is estimated media value calculated, and what would you judge creators on without it?\n- Which creators’ audiences shared and commented the most?\n\nFor renewals, our list of questions to ask before you renew includes what a good answer sounds like. Our guide to which posts work shows how to compare posts fairly.\n\n## What a holdout test looks like with creators\n\nCreators are the hardest channel to test, because organic posts go wherever the algorithm sends them. You can’t switch a creator off in one region. Two approaches work.\nFirst, test the paid part. If you boost creator posts, run a holdout test on the boost: on in some regions, off in matched ones, for a few weeks. That tells you what the spend adds.\nSecond, stagger the creators. Space posts out instead of stacking them in one launch week, and line each one up with your own numbers by day. It isn’t proof the way a regional test is, but it separates creators who move results from creators who post on a busy week.\nWant to see which creators held up? Bring your creator recap to us as your first question, free. We check every code and claim against your own numbers.\n\n# Glossary\n\nAttribution: Deciding which ad, post or channel gets credit for a sale. Every ad platform does its own attribution, and each one gives itself credit. That’s why your agencies’ numbers can add up to more than you sold.\nIncrementality: The sales that happened because of a campaign and wouldn’t have happened without it. If people searching your name would have bought anyway, those sales aren’t incremental, even if an ad was clicked first.\nDouble counting: When two or more agencies or platforms each take credit for the same sale. A buyer who saw a creator video and then clicked a Meta ad can be claimed by both agencies. Add their reports together and you get more sales than you made.\nHoldout test: A test where a campaign runs in some places and not others, so you can compare. Run ads in half your regions for a few weeks and leave the other half alone. The difference in sales is the campaign’s real effect.\nROAS: Return on ad spend: sales credited to ads, divided by what the ads cost. ROAS is only as honest as the sales it’s credited with. If those sales are double counted, ROAS looks better than it is.\nView-through conversion: A sale credited to an ad that someone saw but didn’t click. View-throughs can inflate results fast, because a lot of people see an ad and buy for other reasons.\nConversion window: How many days after seeing or clicking an ad a sale still counts for it. A long window lets an ad claim sales that happened weeks later for other reasons.\nLast-click attribution: Giving all the credit to the last thing someone clicked before buying. It often hands credit to brand search, which mostly catches people who were already going to buy.\nBrand search: Ads shown to people who search your brand’s name. People typing your name were already looking for you. Paying for those clicks can make a channel look great while doing little.\nCreator code: A discount code or link given to a creator so sales can be traced to them. Codes leak to coupon sites and get used by people who’d have bought anyway, so they need checking against your own numbers.\nFirst-party data: Data your company collects itself: sales, tickets, sign-ups, viewing, customers, email, your own social accounts. It’s the most reliable data you have, and usually the least used.\nThird-party data: Data from outside your company: public conversation, search trends, reviews, market research. On its own it tells you what people say. Lined up with your first-party data, it tells you whether they buy.\nSocial listening: Reading what people say about a brand, product or topic across social media and the web. Most listening tools summarize sentiment. Useful listening counts specific things, like the words fans use, and checks them against sales.\nMedia mix modeling: A statistical model that estimates how much each channel contributes to sales over time. It needs a lot of clean history to work, and a data team to keep it honest.\nShare rate: How often people who saw a post sent it on: shares divided by views. A share means someone put their own name behind your post. It’s often the clearest sign a post has a life beyond the people who saw it.\nEngagement rate: Likes, comments, shares and saves on a post, divided by the people who saw it or by follower count. It tells you how hard a post landed with the people who saw it. It doesn’t tell you how many people that was.\nReach vs. impressions: Reach is how many different people saw something. Impressions is how many times it was shown, counting repeats. Impressions are always the bigger number, which is why they lead so many recaps. Ten impressions can be one person seeing an ad ten times.\nBrand lift study: A survey that compares people who saw a campaign with people who didn’t, to measure changes in awareness, recall or intent. It’s one of the few measures built for brand work. It measures what people say in a survey, which is a step away from what they buy.\nRetail media: Ads sold by retailers on their own websites and apps, measured against the retailer’s own sales data. It’s often the closest a brand gets to seeing ads tied to retail sales. It’s also measured by the company selling the ads.\nSell-through and depletions: Sell-through is how much product shoppers buy at retail. Depletions are how much distributors sell on to retailers, bars and restaurants. For drinks and other retail-heavy brands, these are the numbers closest to real demand. Shipments into a warehouse can rise while shelves sit full.\nCreator whitelisting: Running paid ads through a creator’s account, so the ad appears under their name. Also called allowlisting or partnership ads. Whitelisted posts mix creator content and paid media in one place, which makes it easy for two agencies to claim the same results.\nSentiment: A score for whether conversation about a brand is positive, negative or neutral. Sentiment is the headline of most listening reports. Automated scores miss sarcasm and in-jokes, which is how many audiences talk.\n";
